rage throughout Argentina.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below shows the distribution of the network by jurisdiction as of September 30, 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jurisdiction             Branches     ATMs     Self-Service Terminals   \par City of Buenos Aires     829          2677     1888                     \par Buenos Aires             1492         5206     2803                     \par Catamarca                21           180      27                       \par C\u243\'00\'F3rdoba                  460          1745     544                      \par Corrientes               81           392      70                       \par Chaco                    68           406      73                       \par Chubut                   71           282      92                       \par Entre R\u237\'00\'EDos               130          543      206                      \par Formosa                  29           165      20                       \par Jujuy                    35           225      87                       \par La Pampa                 73           228      28                       \par La Rioja                 28           167      35                       \par Mendoza                  174          675      331                      \par Misiones                 67           344      137                      \par Neuqu\u233\'00\'E9n                  85           367      99                       \par R\u237\'00\'EDo Negro                81           310      149                      \par Salta                    72           398      155                      \par San Juan                 39           223      88                       \par San Luis                 47           190      80                       \par Santa Cruz               51           219      65                       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jurisdiction            Branches     ATMs      Self-Service Terminals   \par Santa Fe                486          1533      1042                     \par Santiago del Estero     58           282       51                       \par Tucum\u225\'00\'E1n                 97           425       164                      \par Tierra del Fuego        25           112       48                       \par Total                   4599         17294     8282                     \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Presentation of Financial Statements in Pesos; Inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historically, inflation in Argentina has played a significant role in influencing the economic conditions in Argentina and, in turn, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial results of companies operating in Argentina, such as Grupo Supervielle.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina has faced and continues to face inflationary pressures. From 2011 to date, Argentina experienced increases in inflation as measured by the WPI that reflected the continued growth in the levels of private consumption and economic activity (including exports and public and private sector investment), which applied upward pressure on the demand for goods and services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During periods of high inflation, effective wages and salaries tend to fall and consumers tend to accelerate their consumption patterns and also eliminate unnecessary expenses. The increase in inflationary risk may erode macroeconomic growth and further limit the availability of financing, causing a negative impact on our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IAS 29 requires that financial statements of any entity whose functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of a hyperinflationary economy, be stated in terms of the measuring unit current at the end of the reporting period. IAS 29 does not establish a set inflation rate beyond which an economy is deemed to be experiencing hyperinflation. However, hyperinflation is commonly understood to occur when changes in price levels are close to or exceed 100% on a cumulative basis over the last three years, along with other several macroeconomic-related qualitative factors. Following this criteria, Argentine economy must be considered hyperinflationary according to IAS 29 starting July 1, 2018. As a consequence, financial statements for the year ended on December 31, 2020 have been stated in terms of the measuring unit current at the consolidated financial statement date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table shows the rate of inflation, as measured by the variations in the WPI and the CPI, according to INDEC and the evolution of the reference stabilization coefficient ('CER,' per its Spanish acronym) index and UVA used to adjust the principal of certain of our assets and liabilities, for the periods indicated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                       Year ended December 31,   \par                       2020                                 2019       2018   \par                                                                              \par                       (in percentages) \par Price Indices:(1)                                                 \par WPI                   35.4%                       58.5%           73.5% \par CPI                   36.1%                       53.8%           47.6% \par Adjustment Index:                                                 \par CER                   36.14%                      51.6%           47.2% \par UVA(2)                36.18%                      51.84%          46.86% \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Source: INDEC, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (2) UVAs are inflation adjusted units introduced in September 2016.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Composition of our Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth our 