separate reserve within equity attributable to owners of the Group.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.5. Segment Reporting\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An operating segment is defined as a component of an entity or a Group that engages in business activities from which it may earn revenues and incur expenses (including revenues and expenses relating to transactions with other components of the same entity), and whose financial information is evaluated on a regular basis by the chief operating decision maker.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating segments are reported in a manner consistent with the internal reporting provided to:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i) Key personnel of the senior management who account for the main authority in operating decision-making processes and is responsible for allocating resources and assessing the performance of operating segments; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ii) The Board, who is in charge of making strategic decisions of the Group.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-20\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.6. Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translation (a) Functional and presentation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Figures included in the Consolidated Financial Statements of each of the Group's entities are measured using the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , that is, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the primary economic environment in which the entity operates. Consolidated Financial Statements are presented in Argentine pesos, which is the functional and presentation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Group.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Transactions and balances\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Transactions in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are translated into the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  using the exchange rates published by the Argentine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the dates of the transactions. Gains and losses in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  resulting from the settlement of such transactions and from the translation of monetary assets and liabilities denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at year end exchange rates, are recognized in the income statement, under 'Exchange rate differences on gold and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ', except when such items are deferred in the shareholders' equity for transactions classified as cash flow hedges, when applicable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020 and 2019, the balances in US dollars were converted at the reference exchange rate determined by the Argentine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In the case of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than US dollars, they have been converted to this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  using the rexchange rates derived from repo transactions reported by the Argentine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.7. Cash and due from banks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and due from Banks includes available cash and unrestricted deposits held in Banks, which are short-term liquid instruments and have original maturities of less than three months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Assets disclosed under cash and due from Banks are measured at amortized cost which is close to its fair value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and Cash equivalents include cash and highly liquid short-term securities with an original maturity of less than three-months according to the following detail:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Item                                                     12/31/2020       12/31/2019       12/31/2018   \par Cash and due from banks                                  36,674,869       35,945,335       70,551,283   \par Debt securities at fair value through profit or loss     1,868,604        773,961          26,458,009   \par Money Market Funds(*)                                    923,053          1,313,597        1,372,931    \par Cash and cash equivalents                                39,466,526       38,032,893       98,382,223   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (*) Included within the 'other financial assets' line in the statement of financial position\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group invests in money market funds (MMF) whose investments qualify individually as cash and cash equivalents. An MMF is an open-ended mutual fund that invests in short-term debt instruments (typically one day to one year) such as treasury bills, certificates of deposit, bonds, government gilts and commercial papers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These MMF have to comply with strict fund policies such as:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Controls ensuring constant net asset value or linear performance to limit volatility supported by actual performance; - Returns benchmarked to short-term money market interest rates; - Investment in high-quality instruments with high liquidity and a maximum weighted average maturity of a few weeks; and - Highly diversified portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reconciliation between balances as appearing on the Statement of Financial Position and the items in the Statement of Cash Flow:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Items                                      12/31/2020       12/31/2019       12/31/2018   \par Cash and due from Banks                                  