        8,163,994        -                -                8,163,994    \par Total Liabilities                                          8,422,054        -                -                8,422,054    \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-49\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Below is shown the reconcilation of the financial instruments classiffied as Fair Value Level 3:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FV level 3                                                 12/31/2019       Transfers       Additions       Disposals       P/L       12/31/2020   \par Assets                                                                                                                                             \par - Debt securities at fair value through profit or loss     4,998            -               -               4,998           -         -            \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's policy is to recognize transfers between fair value levels only at end of period. The transfers were produced by the classification as Level 3 of the financial instruments with lack of observable prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Valuation Techniques\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Valuation techniques to determine fair values Level 2 and Level 3 include the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Market or quoted prices for similar instruments. - The estimated present value of instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The valuation technique to determine fair value Level 2 is based on inputs other than the quoted price included in Level 1 that are readily observable for the asset or liability (i.e., prices).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Level 3, the Group uses valuation techniques through spot rate curves which calculate the yield upon market prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These valuation techniques are detailed below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Interpolation model: It consists of the determination of the value of financial instruments that do not have a market price at the closing date, based on quoted prices for similar assets (both in terms of issue, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and duration) in the active markets ( MAE, Bolsar or secondary) through the linear interpolation of them. This technique has been used by the Entity to determine the fair value of the instruments issued by the BCRA and Treasury Bills without quotation at the end of this period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Performance Curve Model under Nelson Siegel: This model proposes a continuous function to model the trajectory of the instant forward interest rate considering as a domain the term comprised until the next interest and / or capital payment. It consists in the determination of the instrument's price estimating volatility through market curves. The Entity has used this model to estimate prices in debt securities or financial instruments with variable interest rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The principal inputs considered by the Group for its determination of fair values \u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0Bunder the linear interpolation model are:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Instrument prices that were quoted between the date the curve is estimated and the settlement date of the latest payment available.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Implicit rates in the last available tender.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Only instruments that have been traded with a 24-hour settlement are considered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - If the same instrument has been listed on MAE ('Mercado Abierto Electr\u243\'00\'F3nico') and Bolsar, only the market price that has been traded in the market with higher volume is considered\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The yield curve is standardized based on a set of nodes, each of which has an associated expiration date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Instruments denominated in US dollars are converted at the exchange rate on the date the instrument is negotiated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-50\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Likewise, for the determination of fair values \u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0Bunder the Nelson Siegel model, the main data and aspects considered by the Entity were:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The Spot rate curves in pesos + BADLAR and the Spot rate curve in US dollars are established based on bonds predefined by Financial Risk Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The main source of prices for Bonds is MAE, without considering those corresponding to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for own portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The portfolio of bonds used as input is changed with every issuance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group periodically evaluates the performance of the models based on indicators which have defined tolerance thresholds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under IFRS, the estimated residual value of an instrument at inception is generally the transaction price.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the event that the transaction price differs from the determined fair value, the difference will be recognized in the income statement proportionally for the duration of the instrument.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6.2 Fair Value of other Financial Instruments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following describes the methodologies and assumptions used to determine the fair values \u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0Bof financial instruments not recorded at their value in these financial statements:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Assets whose fair value is similar to book value: For financial assets and liabilities that are liquid or have short-term maturities (less than three months), the book value is considered to be similar to fair value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Fixed rate financial instruments: The fair value of financial assets was determined by discounting future cash flows at the current market rates offered, for each year, for financial instruments with similar characteristics. The estimated fair value of deposits with a fixed interest rate was determined by discounting future cash flows through the use of market interest rates for deposits with maturities similar to those of the Group's portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - For listed assets and the quoted debt, fair value was determined based on market prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Below is the difference between the carrying amount and the fair value of the main assets and liabilities recorded at amortized cost as of December 31, 2020 and 2019, respectively:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Financial Instruments as of 12/31/2020                            Book value        Fair value        FV Level 1       FV Level 2       FV Level 3    \par Financial Assets                                                                                                                                            \par - Cash and due from Banks                                               36,674,869        36,674,869        36,674,869       -                -             \par - Other financial assets                                                877,330           877,330           877,330          -                -             \par - Loans and other financing                                             105,395,186       112,402,330       -                -                112,402,330   \par - Reverse Repo transactions                                             22,354,735        22,354,735        22,354,735       -                -             \par - Other Debt Securities                                                 6,325,194         6,325,194         6,325,194        -                -             \par 