\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Iris Speroni interview: Political caste does not allow Argentina to be\b0\par\par\uc2 1,980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 At the second meeting of the conference cycle entitled "Challenges for Agro-Industry and National Power" of the New Argentina program, causa Argentina interviewed economist Iris Speroni (@SperoniIris), who holds a Postgraduate Degree in Agribusiness and Master in Finance, in order to understand what are the tools of the current political-economic system that impedes the growth of the country.\par \par \uc2 Although governments on duty, whether Kirchnerism or Macrism, take refuge by blaming the "international context", Speroni describes argentina's political system as "the sand in the engine" that locks Argentina away from its development, especially in agribusiness.\par \par \uc2 "It's a system where politicians work sucking energy (savings or profits) from the collective to keep it a small group, which doesn't invest it in the country itself, but steals that money and buys apartments in Miami. A society that does not reinvest is getting poorer. [...] The system also has mechanisms to avoid being removed or replaced: from '83 to date come peronists and radicals, but the dance is the same, they have all the money to affirm their continuity".\par \par \uc2 On the myth "Campo vs Industria" discussed in the previous cycle with the engineer Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Alvarez (@Bumpercrop1), where it is called into question whether there can be a model that combines the two sectors in the development and growth of the country, Speroni brings down the myth of agro-exporting Argentina: "Argentina does not export". In international standards, an exporting country pours between 30 and 50% of its production abroad. In the case of Argentina, no more than 20 or 22% is exported. Of the little that is exported, agro-industrial products stand out.\par \par \uc2 Regarding industry, the economist comments that Argentina "could be one of the largest exporting countries of industrial products, as there are constantly people designing and producing new machinery, new products (glass fibers, alloys, material engineering) and all kinds of innovations."\par \par \uc2 But Argentina's tax system, the customs system, and what Speroni describes as "the worst and biggest lock of all" which is the late exchange rate, prevents any production from being viable.\par \par \par \par \uc2 For this, it exemplifies what happens with aluminum. 80% of the aluminium produced is exported in the form of ingots. If, for example, Aluar wanted to sell ingots to an Argentine factory, it has to pay gross revenue on that sale, which is not the case when exporting, so greater export gains are received. On the other hand, if a company wanted to produce aluminum openings or doors for construction in Quilmes, it has to pay more expensive aluminum than the price that came with it imported in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the excuses used by Kirchnerism, these structural costs have nothing to do with the international situation, but are subject to what the country does in terms of domestic policy: these are problems created by several years of politicians who do not live up to the situation, or who for personal gain make decisions that harm the whole country. The clearest example is the country's transport, with railways and shipyards dismantled, and strong leadership of truckers.\par \par \uc2 The economist mentions that Argentina should be one of the leading exporters of agricultural machinery, because it is one of the few countries with more than 100 years of history manufacturing machinery, along with the United States. However, with an exchange rate delayed to $90 and the entire tax burden (gross income, check tax, social charges, etc.), it is impossible to export. An example of this is with Mendoza's wine industry, which today cannot cover their own costs.\par \par \uc2 On the late exchange rate, Iris explains that "the state delivers reserves for private people to buy dollars at $90," and "a dollar backwards has been coming for more than a decade."\par \par \uc2 In legal matters, Iris comments that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is confiscating a good, since according to the Argentine Civil Code, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are goods. "The state does not allow you to collect your good, but forces you to forcibly sell it to him, who will pay you in pesos. That's already illegal because it's a confiscation without prior law." In addition, the state pays those dollars at $90, which is not the market price, this being against the Constitution because any confiscation must be by prior law and at market value.\par \par \uc2 "Whoever produces the exported does not receive all the full money, because instead of paying him $155 per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives him $90, and the difference is left. Last year, $56 billion in exports was sold, of which $28 billion was left by producers and the other US$28 billion remained at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes advantage of that money and sells it to importers, oil companies working in Vaca Muerta such as Total, \uc2 BP\uc2 , or miners who take gold out of the mountain range and are 100% foreign, and buy all their machinery at $90 a dollar, that is, a dollar given away by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , financed by the work of the producers they export. On this scheme, he also comments that the greatest theft is presented in the automotive industry.\par \par \uc2 Iris also denounces that the remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold to a few friends of power that take them abroad. "The $28 billion loot is so large that they have to hand out money and ensure system continuity. That's why it doesn't matter if Cristina comes with Kicillof, Macri with Dujovne, or Alberto with Guzman, everyone does the same. Because the big deal is buying cheap dollars, condemning all Argentine provinces to poverty," he says.\par \par \uc2 Exchange rate evolution since the return of restrictions at the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 With this scheme, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes the visible face of a private appropriation of multimillion-dollar resources, in the hands of the real political oligarchy that dominates our country, "and that is not the 1000 families that say some," he says with laughter.\par \par \uc2 Speroni argues that "a high dollar is Federalism, and a low dollar is unitarism," because a low dollar takes resources away from the provinces to transfer them to the Capital or central power.\par \par \uc2 For example, with a high dollar, Corrientes might not only be exporting wood, but also furniture or openings, the Chaco could industrialize its cotton production, the northwest could export cheese or goat's milk, and so for each exporting province, but with a dollar under companies never earn enough to accumulate capital that they can reinvest.\par \par \uc2 This whole system ends up making the provinces dependent on the gifts that the central power revolves, strengthening it even further, and as a consequence no one emerges, from traditional parties at least, to go out and argue in favor of their province, that wealth should not be transferred to central power through the low exchange rate, but need a high exchange rate that allows their province to export and be richer.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, politicians often have a "perfect excuse" to justify the exchange rate delay: if the exchange rate rises, the price of food rises. Even if they repeat it as a mantra to install it in society, in reality half the price of food is imposed (direct, to fuel, social charges, etc.).\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the exportable staple that any edible product has is a small part of the total. On this, Speroni explains that the price of 1kg of Milanese involves freight from the field to the refrigerator and from the refrigerator to the butcher's shop, with 55% taxes on fuels and tolls, and both establishments must keep cold that is achieved with electricity, which has 40% tax, in addition to municipal, safety and hygiene fees, gross income, among others. "When you look at how important the international price of meat is to 1 kg of Milanese, it's 11%."\par \par \uc2 Consulted regarding the green agenda of sustainable development, which \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  accepts and validates without hesitation, the economist comments that it is part of what she always calls "the \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervened Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 "This radicalism and Kirchnerism, calling itself Peronism, which are actually ideologically radical (including in this group also the Civic Coalition and the PRO), have taken into their hands the global agenda and fulfilled it to the point. [...] herecome with any international agenda and impose it. I believe that within that is abortion, euthanasia, among others."\par \par \uc2 On the green agenda, Iris comments that "the Paris Agreement was signed in Cristina's last years and the first thing Macri did when he took over was to approve it."\par \par \uc2 "This demonstrates the continuity of Argentina's submission to these international agreements. The green agenda is very serious for us, because that's going to increase the cost of energy for a country with oil and gas. What has been invented so far of solar or wind energy, at the moment is very expensive, except exceptional cases where one is isolated and can have energy autonomy."\par \par \uc2 Everything called "the green economy" is largely funded by both the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , which spend billions of dollars on subsidies, and also give money to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  and the United Nations to do the same.\par \par \uc2 "When you look at The \uc2 World Bank\uc2 's programs, millions of dollars are available for green energy development, which are then left to 2 or 3 companies," says Iris, in reference to cases where they were delivered to large state contractors or multinationals such as \uc2 Shell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speroni also comments that "these policies impoverish the population, because when some country announces that from 2030 it will stop producing combustion cars to replace them with electric ones, they immediately lower the resale value to all the cars that people have."\par \par \uc2 New cars, as well as electric cars, are now very expensive and many people find it impossible to access one. If you get so much more engaged in buying a car, people won't be able to change them, staying with their old cars that pollute much more than new ones. At the end of the day, what they say is to improve the environment, ends up harming it, as well as impoverishing the population.\par \par \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  and his environmental agenda.\par \par \uc2 On one of the biggest problems facing our country years ago, Iris states that "it would be interesting if we had trade unionists with political opinion again. Having unionists who talk about gender is pretty pathetic. Having half the population working in black is a goal of political caste, which doesn't want people protected by a union."\par \par \uc2 Stressing the importance of the high exchange rate, Speroni states that "trade unions of all activities should also be able to triple the production of exportable agricultural machinery, but they do not know how to link that to the exchange rate", and then they do not demand it, either because they are busy in their survival, lack of political culture, or , smooth and flat convenience.\par \par \uc2 At the conclusion of the interview, Speroni invites Argentines to have the will to break the lie that the high exchange rate raises the price of food, and defend their own interests.\par \par \uc2 "I see no other way than to take our rightful place as citizens and take charge of our homeland. [...] The National Constitution says that the defense of sovereignty is the responsibility of citizens. The homeland has to defend it ourselves, no one will come to do it in our place," Iris closes.\par \par \uc2 The task of Argentines is to match that of international organizations, going to each place and forming opinion, showing that things can really be solved. They must occupy spaces of power and not stop before those who claim to represent them, because they have not been doing so.\par \par \uc2 Full interview - Iris SperoniBy Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Pierri (CAUSA Argentina), for La Right Diario.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210507eh56000mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
