ssed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7. FINANCE LEASES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7.1 The Group as lessee (i) The following table shows the carrying amount in the statement of financial position: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                        12/31/2020       12/31/2019   \par Right-of-use asset                                   \par Land and buildings     2,146,928        2,047,260    \par Lease liability                                      \par Current                660,694          637,106      \par Non-current            521,004          651,313      \par Total                  1,181,698        1,288,419    \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ii) The following table shows the amounts charged in the income statement: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Items                                                                 12/31/2020   \par Right-of-use assets - Depreciation                                                 780,397   \par Interest expenses on lease liabilities (Other operating expenses)                  207,035   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (iii) Lease activities:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group leases several branches. Rental agreements are generally made for fixed periods of 1 to 3 years, but may have extension options as described in (iv) below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contracts may contain lease components or not. The Group assigns consideration in the contract to the lease and non-lease components based on their independent relative prices. However, for the leases of real estate for which the Group is a lessee, it has chosen not to separate the lease components and those that are not, and instead counts them as a single lease component.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lease terms are negotiated individually and contain a wide range of different terms and conditions. Lease agreements do not impose other obligations to do or not do, other than the leased assets owned by the lessor. Leased assets cannot be used as collateral for obtaining loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until 2018, Property, Plant and Equipment leases were classified as operating leases. As of January 1, 2019, leases are recognized as a right-of-use asset by registering a liability as a counterparty on the date on which the leased asset is available for use by the Entity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Assets and liabilities arising from leases are initially measured based on the present value. Lease liabilities include the net present value of the following lease payments:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 fixed payments (including fixed payments in substance), less any incentives receivable; \u8226\'20\'22 variable lease payments that depend on an index or a rate, initially measured using the index or rate as at the commencement date; \u8226\'20\'22 amounts expected to be payable by the Group under residual value guarantees; \u8226\'20\'22 the exercise price of a purchase option if the Group is reasonably certain to exercise that option, and \u8226\'20\'22 payments of penalties for terminating the lease, if the lease term reflects the Group exercising an option to terminate the lease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lease payments to be made under reasonably certain extension options are also included in the measurement of the liability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-53\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lease payments are discounted using the interest rate implicit in the lease. If that rate cannot be easily determined, which is generally the case with leases in the Group, the lessee's incremental borrowing rate is used, which is the rate that the individual lessee would have to pay to borrow the necessary funds to obtain an asset of similar value to the asset by right of use in a similar economic environment with similar terms, security and conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To determine the incremental interest rate, the Group:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 whenever possible, uses the external financing recently received as a starting point, adjusted to reflect changes in financing conditions since the external financing was received. \u8226\'20\'22 uses a rate determination approach that begins with a risk-free interest rate adjusted for credit risk for leases that the Entity already has for those cases in which it does not have recent third-party financing, and \u8226\'20\'22 makes specific adjustments for the lease, for example, term, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and guarantee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group is exposed to possible future increases in variable lease payments based on an index or rate, which are not included in the lease liability until they become effective. When adjustments to lease payments based on an index or rate become effective, the lease liability is reassessed and adjusted against the right-of-use asset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lease payments are allocated between capital and financial cost. The financial cost is charged to income during the lease period to produce a constant periodic interest rate on the remaining balance of the liability for each period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The right-of-use assets are measured at cost comprising the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 the amount of the initial measurement of the lease liability; \u8226\'20\'22 any lease payment made at or before the commencement date, less any lease incentives received; \u8226\'20\'22 any initial direct costs, and \u8226\'20\'22 an estimate of costs to be incurred by the lessee in dismantling and removing the underlying asset, restoring the site on which it is located or restoring the underlying asset to the condition required by the terms and conditions of the lease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The right-of-use assets are generally depreciated during the shortest useful life of the asset and the lease term in a linear fashion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments associated with short-term leases of equipment and all leases of low-value assets are recognized linearly as an expense in income. Short-term leases are leases with a lease term of 12 months or less and that does not contains a purchase option. Low-value assets include computer equipment and small items of office furniture.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (iv) Extension and termination options\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Extension and termination options are included in several property leases. These are used to maximize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility in terms of managing the assets used in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Most of the extension and termination options maintained are exercisable only by the Group and not by the respective lessor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7.2 The Group as lessor\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following is a breakdown of the maturities of the Group's financial and operating leases receivables and of the current values \u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0Bas of December 31, 2020 and 2019:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial Lease Receivables          12/31/2020         12/31/2019   \par Up to 1 year                         1,982,763          2,427,531    \par More than a year up to two years     1,099,924          1,589,098    \par From two to three years              634,899            906,156      \par From three to five years             355,885            574,490      \par More than five years                 14,429             27,744       \par Total                                4,087,900          5,525,019    \par Unearned financial income            (1,196,789   )     (1,186,642   ) \par Net investment in the lease          2,891,111          4,338,377    \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-54\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The balance of allowance for loan losses related to finance leases amounts to 252,961 and 111,708 as of December 31, 2020 and 2019.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating Lease Receivables          12/31/2019       12/31/2018   \par Up to 1 year                         16,067           22,744       \par More than a year up to two years     13,766           21,418       \par From two to three years              9,202            18,438       \par From three to five years             -                12,528       \par Total                                39,035           75,128       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8. TRANSFER OF FINANCIAL ASSETS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the Group transfers financial assets under an agreement that meets all requirements to derecognize such assets, the difference between the carrying amount of those assets and the amount received as consideration is charged to income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) Transfers that do not qualify for derecognition\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following is a detail of the financial assets transferred by the Group that continue to be recognized in its consolidated financial statements as of December 31, 2020 and 2019:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                            12/31/2020         12/31/2019   \par Securitized Personal Loans                                                 \par Asset                                                   -                    2,197,444   \par Liabilities                                             -                    1,156,889   \par Transfers of receivables with recourse                                                   \par Asset                                                   -                    41,116      \par Liabilities                                             -                    -           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b) Transfers of financial assets that qualify for derecognition\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group makes, in certain opportunities, non-recourse portfolio sales. In these cases, the Group has not retained any substantial risk or reward regarding the transferred portfolio, and therefore, such portfolio meets derecognition requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9. REPO AND REVERSE REPO TRANSACTIONS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group carries out repo transactions in which it performs the spot sale of a security with the related forward purchase thereof, thus substantially retaining all the risks and rewards associated with the instruments and recognizing them in 'Repo Transactions' at year-end, as the provisions set out in point 3.4.2 (Derecognition of Assets) of IFRS 9 'Financial Instruments') are not met.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The residual values \u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0Bof assets transferred under repo and reverse repo transactions as of December 31, 2020 and 2019 are detailed below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Repo Transactions:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                       Book Value   \par December 31, 2020     -            \par December 31, 2019     435,401      \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reverse Repo Transactions:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                       Book Value   \par December 31, 2020     22,354,735   \par December 31, 2019     -            \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-55\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRUPO SUPERVIELLE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020, presented in comparative format\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Expressed in thousands of pesos)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10. DERIVATIVE FINANCIAL INSTRUMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of business, the Group enters into a variety of transactions principally in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stock markets. Most counterparties in the derivative transactions are banks and other financial institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These instruments include:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Forwards and futures: they are agreements to deliver or take delivery at a specified rate, price or index applied against the underlying asset or financial instrument, at a specific date. Futures are exchange traded at standardized amounts of the underlying asset or financial instrument.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forwards contracts are OTC agreements and are principally dealt in by the Group in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as forward agreements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Swaps: they are agreements between two parties with the intention to exchange cash flows and risks at specific date and for a period in the future. - Options: they confer the right to the buyer, but no obligation, to receive or pay a specific quantity of an asset or financial instrument for a specified price at or before a specified date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2020 and 2019, the following amounts were recorded for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  related to derivatives:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                             12/31/2020               12/31/2019   \par Amounts receivable for spot and forward transactions pending settlement                  143,944                    350,680   \par Amounts payable for spot and forward transactions pending settlement                     -                          -         \par                                                                                          143,944                    350,680   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table shows, the notional value of options and outstanding forward and futures contracts as of December 31, 2020 and 2019:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                              