lectric power, see 'Operating and Financial Review and Prospects-Factors Affecting our Results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Our Revenues' and 'Item 4.B. Business Overview-The Argentine Electric Power Sector-Structure of the Industry.'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seasonality\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seasonality of Electricity Generation by Thermal Facilities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graphic breaks down our average thermal energy production from our continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the last four years on a month-by-month basis:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: CAMMESA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seasonality of Water Resources and Electricity Generation of Piedra del \u193\'00\'C1guila\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The availability of water is the key factor for determining Piedra de \u193\'00\'C1guila's electric power generation capacity and is tied to annual and seasonal changes in rains in the upstream mountain area of Piedra del \u193\'00\'C1guila. Water levels generally increase between May and December due to the winter rains and the spring thaw, and we are able to produce more energy over such periods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graphic breaks down our average hydroelectric energy production over the last four years on a month-by-month basis:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: CAMMESA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seasonality of Wind Resources and Electricity Generation of Achiras La Castellana I, La Castellana II, Manque and La Genoveva II wind farms\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The availability of wind resources is the key factor for determining Achiras I and La Castellana I wind farms electric power generation capacity and is tied to annual and seasonal changes in wind speed in the areas where each farm is located. Wind speed is generally higher between May and September and we are able to produce more energy over such periods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graphic shows the energy production of our wind farms on a month-by-month since inception and until December 2020:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Competition\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The demand for electric power in Argentina is served by a variety of generation companies, both state-owned and private-owned. These companies pursue the right to supply generation capacity and electric power and to develop projects to serve the demand for electric power in Argentina. Some of our foreign competitors are substantially larger and have substantially greater resources than our company. Because of the significant gap between the demand and supply of electric power in Argentina, voluntary and forced blackouts at times of seasonal peak consumption have occurred. During 2018 (and particularly, on February 28, 2018, pursuant to CAMMESA's records), there was a historic demand peak recorded totaling 26.3 GW, which was supplied by 24.6 GW of capacity domestically (out of a total installed capacity of 36.9 GW) without energy imports from neighboring countries.In 2020, 1,204 GWh of energy were imported, representing a 56% decrease in energy imports as compared to 2019.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our primary competitors in the electric power generation market are the Enel group, AES Argentina Generaci\u243\'00\'F3n S.A. (an affiliate of the AES Corporation), Pampa Energ\u237\'00\'EDa S.A, and YPF EE S.A.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Below we detail the installed capacity of the main private sector generators in Argentina, as of December 31 2020:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Company and subsidiaries Power (MW)\par Central Puerto           4,709(1)(2)\par The AES Corporation      4,348(3)\par Grupo Enel               4,015(4)(2)\par Pampa Energ\u237\'00\'EDa S.A.       4,774(5)\par YPF EE                   2,610(6)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: (1) Based on the documentation officially declared to CAMMESA by Central Puerto for continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (2) Based on company's financial statements as of and for the year ended December 31, 2020 and data from CAMMESA. (3) Based on data form CAMMESA. Accounts for only assets located in Argentina, operated by any of the following subsidiaries: AES Argentina Generaci\u243\'00\'F3n S.A., AES Juramento S.A., AES Alicura S.A., AES Parana S.A. and Termoandes S.A. (4) Based on data from CAMMESA. Includes Enel Generaci\u243\'00\'F3n Costanera S.A., Enel Generaci\u243\'00\'F3n El Choc\u243\'00\'F3n S.A. and a 40.00% stake of Grupo Enel in Central Dock Sud S.A. (5) Based on company's financial statements as of and for the year ended on December 31, 2019. (6) Based on data from CMMESA.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graphs break down the market share of electric power generation supplied by private sector companies in Argentina as of December 31, 2020, based on data published by CAMMESA:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: CAMMESA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graphs break down total market share of electric power generation supply and the market share from thermal units (from both private and public sector generators) in Argentina as of December 31, 2020, based on data published by CAMMESA:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: CAMMESA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: CAMMESA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table shows the installed capacity in terms of MW assigned to each regulatory framework (Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base, Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Plus, Resolution No. 220/07) for us and each of our main competitors as of December 31, 2020:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In MW                 Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base(Res. 1/2019 and 1/2020) Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Plus (Res. 1281/06) Power Purchase Agreements Under Res. 220/07 Power Purchase Agreements Under Res. 21/17 Power Purchase Agreements Under Res. 287/17 Term market for renewable energy (MATER) RenovAr Total\par Central Puerto(1)     3.652                                16                          280                                         -               