               -            578,393      N/A       N/A\par Net income for the year                                           6,957,938               11,790,940   35,991,177   (40.99%)  (67.24%)\par                                                                   \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenues from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2                                                   Year ended December 31, Change December 31,\par                                                   2020                    2019       2018       2020/2019 2019/2018\par                                                   (in thousands of Ps.)   (in percentages)\par Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base (1)                                  17,473,763              37,273,808 26,530,179 (53.12%)  40.50%\par Sales under contracts(2)                          18,395,788              10,007,294 1,878,157  83.82%    432.83%\par Steam sales(3)                                    1,064,771               591,732    515,089    79.94%    14.88%\par Resale of gas transport and distribution capacity 394,841                 389,746    406,058    1.31%     (4.02%)\par Revenues from CVO thermal plant management        778,997                 694,643    546,244    12.14%    27.17%\par Total revenues from ordinary activities           38,108,160              48,957,223 29,875,727 (22.16%)  63.87%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Includes (i) sales of energy and power to CAMMESA remunerated under Resolution No. 95, Resolution No. 19/2017, Res. SE 1/2019 and Res.SE 31/2020 (ii) spot sales of energy and power to CAMMESA not remunerated under Resolution No. 95 (as amended), (iii) remuneration under Resolution No. 724/2008 relating to agreements with CAMMESA to improve existing Argentine power generation capacity and (iv) income under Res. SEE 70/18 (see 'Item 4.B. Business Overview-The Argentine Electric Power Sector-Remuneration Scheme-The Previous Remuneration Schemes-Resolution SEE 70/18-Option to purchase fuel for units under Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base Regulatory Framework.').\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Includes (i) term market sales under contracts and, (ii) energy sold under the Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Plus, (iii ) contracts under the MATER framework and (iv) RenovAr Program sales under contracts (for further information regarding term market sales under contract, see 'Item 4.B. Business Overview-Our Customers') (for further information regarding term market sales under contract, see 'Item 4.B. Business Overview-Our Customers').\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Includes steam sold under steam sale contract with YPF from the Luj\u225\'00\'E1n de Cuyo Plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2020 Compared to 2019\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenues from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2020 totaled Ps.38.11 billion, a 22.16% decrease from Ps. 48.96 billion in 2019. This decrease was primarily attributable to:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the abrogation of Resolution No. 70/2018 mentioned above, on December 30, 2019, which resulted on fuel remuneration for units under Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base regulatory framework (and other related concepts) amounting to Ps. 1.3 billion during 2020, compared to Ps. 14.9 billion in 2019 (when Res. 70/18 was in force). Also, revenues were negatively impacted by a price reduction due to Res. 31/2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Excluding fuel remuneration, revenues for 2020 would have been Ps. 36.8 billion, an 8% increase compared to Ps. 34.1 billion of 2019. This increase was mainly due to:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An 83.82% increase in sales under contracts, which amounted to Ps. 18.4 billion during the 2020, as compared to Ps. 10 billion in 2019, mainly due to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the full period of La Castellana II (15.2 MW), La Genoveva II (41.8 MW) wind farms that commenced their commercial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the 3Q2019, and Manque (57 MW), Los Olivos (22.8 MW) and La Genoveva I (88.2 MW), which started \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during December 2019 (partially), February, and November 2020, respectively. Also due to Brigadier Lopez and Lujan de Cuyo's COD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An 79.94% increase in the steam sales, mainly due to the new Luj\u225\'00\'E1n de Cuyo cogeneration unit, which totaled Ps. 1.1 billion in 2020, compared to Ps. 0.6 billion of 2019.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This increase was partially offset by a decrease in spot sales/Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base (Revenues from Resolution 1, Resolution 31, Resolution 19, SGE Resolution 70/2018 and amendments) which, without considering the remuneration associated to the self-procured fuel under Res. 70/18 mentioned above, were Ps. 16.2 billion in 2020 as compared to Ps. 22.4 billion in 2019, mainly due to the a decrease in prices for units under the Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base Regulatory framework established by Res. 31/2020, in force since February 1, 2020, and the suspension of Annex VI of such resolution (monthly price adjustment procedure) instructed by the Secretariat of Energy to CAMMESA on April 8, 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To a lesser extent, spot sales/Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base were also affected by (i) a decrease in machine availability for thermal units which was 89% during 2020, compared to 93% in 2019, mainly due to a significative failure in the main transformer of the Siemens branded combined cycle which returned to service on July 16, 2020, some steam turbines from Lujan de Cuyo operated most of the year with power limitation and certain failures in the combined cycle of Puerto's complex during June and October 2020, and the unavailability for some of the steam turbines of the Puerto, (ii) a 12% decrease in energy generation form the hydro plant Piedra del \u193\'00\'C1guila due to lower waterflow in the Limay and Coll\u243\'00\'F3n Cur\u225\'00\'E1 rivers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2019 Compared to 2018\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenues from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2019 totaled Ps. 48.96 billion, a 63.87 % increase from Ps. 29.88 billion in 2018. This increase was primarily attributable to:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1. A 40.50% increase in our revenues from electric power sold under Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base, which amounted to Ps. 37.27 billion during the year ended December 31, 2019, compared to Ps. 26.53 billion during the year ended December 31, 2018, mainly explained by (i) an increase in the exchange rate for 2019 higher than the inflation for the period, which impacted directly on tariffs set in U.S. dollars, in terms of Argentine pesos current at the end of the reporting period. As a reference, during 2019, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate increased 58.86 % and the inflation rate was 53.83%, while during 2018 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate increased 102.2% and the inflation rate was 47.64%, (ii) an increase in fuel remuneration for units under Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base regulatory framework (and other related concepts), which amounted to Ps. 14.88 billion during the year ended December 31, 2019 (see 'Item 5.A.-Factors Affecting Our Results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Our Revenues-The Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base'), compared to Ps. 4.07 billion during the year ended December 31, 2018. The increase in our revenues from electric power sold under Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Base was partially offset by the tariff decrease established by Res. 1/19, which set lower prices for energy generation and generation availability than from that applied during 2018.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2. a 432.83% increase in our revenues from sales under contracts (including the Energ\u237\'00\'EDa Plus contracts, which are denominated in U.S. dollars), which amounted to Ps. 10.01 billion during the year ended December 31, 2019, compared to Ps. 1.88 billion during the year ended December 31, 2018, primarily attributable to (a) Ps. 4.74 billion in revenues from the Brigadier Lopez plant, which we acquired in 2019, (b) the commencement of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during 2019 of the co-generation project Luj\u225\'00\'E1n de Cuyo, which generated revenues totaling Ps. 797.62 million, (c) the commencement of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during 2019 of the wind farm projects La Castellana II, La Genoveva II and Manque, which generated Ps. 533.45 million in revenues, in the aggregate, and (d) a 58.86% devaluation of the peso with respect to the U.S. dollar in 2019, offset by an inflation rate of 53.83% during 2019, while during 2018 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate increased 102.2% and the inflation rate was 47.64%;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3. a 14.88% increase in our revenues from steam sales to YPF from our Luj\u225\'00\'E1n de Cuyo Plant, which amounted to Ps. 591.73 million during the year ended December 31, 2019, compared to Ps. 515.09 million during the year ended December 31, 2018, primarily attributable to a 58.86% devaluation of the peso with respect to the U.S. dollar in 2019, offset by an inflation rate of 53.83% during 2019, while during 2018 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate increased 102.2% and the inflation rate was 47.64%, and by a 6.48% decrease in the quantity of steam sold (1,031,044 tons in 2019 as compared to 1,102,515 tons in 2018).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cost of Sales from continuing \f28 \b \high