\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Working from home to the outside: what the "crypto" trick is like to bring dollars to blue value\b0\par\par\uc2 2,242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Program, design, write or translate. They do marketing, engineering or customer service tasks. They live in Argentina, but work abroad. These freelancers make in dollars. But then, by bringing their money into the country, the "cepo" forces them to charge in pesos at the officialvalue, with a heavy loss compared to the blue quote. To avoid this, the "crypto world" and its legal "greys" are increasingly used.\par \par \uc2 The difference is remarkable. By normal means, a $500 payment received by the freelancer abroad translates to just over $46,000 when you arrive in the country, which then applies fees and withholdings.\par \par \uc2 But if the trade is done with cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin, USDC or DAI, the worker can have about $75 thousand in hand to face their daily expenses in Argentina. Or you can keep the dollars.\par \par \uc2 Such a gap has driven many to choose this novel route of collection, which takes advantage of the lack of digital asset regulations to move them sideways to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But what is this crypto "trick" by which more and more freelancers manage to bring to the country what they earn abroad at market value? Through which sites is it done?\u160\'00\'A0And what legal and tax consequences can it bring? In this article, the answers.\par \par \uc2 Neither dollars nor euros: transferring crypto-assetsThe manoeuvre in question is not to bring to the country directly the dollars or euros received abroad for the task carried out. What freelancers do is:\par \par \uc2 1ST) GET A CRYPTOCURRENCY "WALLET". It is like a kind of international personal account, with a unique address, that allows you to send, receive and treasure digital assets. You can open a free and cost-free maintenance at multiple sites (some are detailed below).\par \par \uc2 2O) CHARGE IN CRYPTOCURRENCIES FOR WORK. The teleworker asks your client from abroad to pay you not with dollars or euros,but by transferring to your wallet an amount of cryptocurrencies equivalent to those dollars or euros.\par \par \uc2 3RD) SELL THE CRYPTOCURRENCIES IN ARGENTINA. The "digital dollars" received are offered in the local cryptocurrency market to people interested in obtaining them, who will deliver dollars or pesos at a value similar to that of the blue market.\par \par \uc2 All this "rulo" may seem complicated, but there are currently platforms that allow anyone to do it in minutes without the need for expert knowledge.\par \par \uc2 In "the cloud," above bordersWhat is novel and disruptive is that this avoids an international transfer or shipment of common remittances, with all the costs and controls that this entails.\par \par \uc2 It happens that, technically, with this maneuver there are no longer dollars traveling from a bank account in country A to another bank account in a country B.\par \par \uc2 What there are are are encrypted digital codes - to which the market recognizes some value - moving between "virtual wallets" that are not in certain countries. They're in "the cloud."\par \par \uc2 The "wallet" of origin (of the employer) and the destination (of the freelancer) are in distributed and decentralized global networks that no government or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can control or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Similarly, then, already in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , someone will pay the freelancer pesos or dollars - in cash or with a normal transfer - for the teleworker to forward the cryptocurrencies to him, again within "the cloud".\par \par \uc2 What cryptocurrencies are used to "bring dollars" to the country?The best known cryptocurrency is Bitcoin (BTC) and some choose it. The problem is that your quote against the dollar can vary greatly from time to time unpredictably,due to supply or demand spikes. The same goes for others, like Ethereum? \uc2 Litecoin\uc2  (ETH) and \uc2 Litecoin\uc2  (LTC).\par \par \uc2 Therefore, to avoid surprises, freelancers often use stablecoins or stable cryptocurrencies, the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of which is explained in detail in this article.\par \par \uc2 This category of cryptoactives, which has the USD Coin (USDC), Thether (USDT) and DAI as the main exponents, is distinguished by always staying in parity 1 to 1 with a traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The three cases mentioned are mirrors of thedollar, so they are known as "digital dollars".\par \par \uc2 How and where are cryptocurrencies liquidated to receive blue pesos?When converting Bitcoins, USDC, USDT, DAI or other cryptocurrencies that the worker receives as payment, there are two great ways to sell them in Argentina and receive pesos.\par \par \uc2 1) EXCHANGE PLATFORMS "BETWEEN PARES" OR "P2P"\par \par \uc2 One way is to go to websites that link the one who wants to sell a cryptocurrency with another person from the same country (a couple) who is interested in buying it. They are a kind of"Free Market"crypto.\par \par \uc2 Who, for example, received a payment of 500 Tether (similar to $500)for a job can post on a "P2P" platform that intends to sell them at a certain price in pesos or dollars. And, if there is a buyer interested, the system puts them in touch to make the exchange.\par \par \uc2 One of these platforms is Paxful, which allows you to open for free a "digital wallet" where you can receive payments in Bitcoins, Thether or Ethereum; and then offer the cryptocurrencies to local users willing to buy them with a common transfer or cash.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, in this market, people were willing to pay around $142 for every "digital dollar,"a value very similar to that of the informal dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Paxful allows Argentine self-employed people to receive their payments from abroad in crypto at the equivalent value of the blue dollar," explained Renata Rodrigues, Marketing Manager at Paxful.\par \par \uc2 "If instead chose to collect from a traditional bank account, the entity would settle those dollars at the officialvalue, making about 45% less profit,"he compared.\par \par \uc2 The transaction is between individuals, but the site verifies the identity of all users and ensures that the two parties comply with the agreement.\par \par \uc2 For its services it charges 0.5% of the amount if the cryptocurrency sale is made against bank transfer and 1% if it is cash.\par \par \uc2 2) CRYPTOCURRENCY "CHANGE HOUSES"\par \par \uc2 The other great option, perhaps easier for beginners, is to resort to the so-called digital "exchange houses" that operate in Argentina. Some of them are Ripio, Satoshitango, SeSocio and Buenbit.\par \par \uc2 These exchanges, after registering, provide a virtual "wallet" to receive the cryptocurrencies and then allow them to be converted to pesos or dollars at market price (similar to blue) and instantly,without having to get someone who just wants to buy or wait for them to make a payment.\par \par \uc2 On these sites, having the cryptocurrencies already in the wallet, everything is limited to pressing a "sell" button.\par \par \uc2 The pesos or dollars obtained will be credited immediately and can be transferred on the spot to a common bank account (CBU) or to a virtual wallet (CVU) such as Mercado Pago, Ual\u225\'00\'E1 or Naranja X.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Buenbit\par \par \uc2 "The ease offered by platforms like Buenbit is that it is not dependent on another person and the user can sell the cryptocurrencies instantly, and then transfer pesos or dollars to their account without paying any commission," he explained to Clar\u237\'00\'EDn Emiliano Limia, the firm's spokesman.\par \par \uc2 "The final quote of the entire trade may vary depending on the network fees of each cryptocurrency, the commissions of the platform used (Buenbit does not charge) and the quote of the cryptocurrency with which it is traded," he explained.\par \par \uc2 This Sunday, who sold 1 DAI (equivalent to $1) on the site could get about $151.50. "Between freelancers, interest and the volume of crypto collection is growing," Limia observed.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in SeSocio\par \par \uc2 "Professionals working abroad from Argentina can collect their fees in cryptocurrencies through SeSocio. In 2020, this modality grew, usually using stablecoins," Lucas Marincovich, a cryptocurrency specialist in that field, told Clar\u237\'00\'EDn Lucas Marincovichfintech.\par \par \uc2 How do you do that? After registering and activating the account, they explained, a unique address is generated that operates as a CBU, but to receive the stablecoins. That's the code that must be sent to the customer abroad or employer to direct their cryptocurrency payment there.\par \par \uc2 Once the freelancer receives that payment into his SeSocio account, he can already sell the those cryptocurrencies on the platform to obtain pesos at an exchange rate "close to the value of the dollar counted with local market liquidation," they said. This Friday they paid $150 for every "digital dollar."\par \par \uc2 "Cryptos can also be sold in dollars or used to invest in projects within the platform," Marincovich added. The pesos or dollars obtained, they clarified, can be withdrawn at any time to a traditional bank account.\par \par \uc2 The operative in Satoshitango\par \par \uc2 Many Argentine freelancers also use Satoshitango to receive payments on any of the cryptocurrencies that that platform supports (Bitcoin, Ethereum, DAI, USDC,Ripple, \uc2 Litecoin\uc2  and Bitcoin Cash) and then convert them to local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In the last year these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew by 800% in Argentina compared to the previous year," he revealed to Clar\u237\'00\'EDn Matias Bari, CEO and co-founder of the platform.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the professional must be registered in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.satoshitango.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.satoshitango.com}}}\uc2  and provide their unique address to the person transferring the cryptocurrencies, who must also be a user of the site. "The transfer is free of charge and is done immediately," Bari said.\par \par \uc2 This Friday, on the platform, they paid just over $153 for 1 DAI or $1, stable cryptocurrenciesthat are always worth pretty much the same as $1.\par \par \uc2 What if the foreign customer does not want to pay in cryptocurrencies?In countries where the work of Argentine freelancers, such as the United States and many in Europe, is most often demanded, using dollars and euros to buy cryptocurrencies is legal and has become common.\par \par \uc2 So much so that many companies today offer to pay in cryptocurrencies for work that they outsource to freelancers around the world.\par \par \uc2 They do this to reduce costs and to facilitate collection in countries with harsh exchange restrictions, such as Argentina and Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, in front of an employer that only offers to pay in dollar, euro or similar, what some freelancers do is:\par \par \uc2 Open a bank account or virtual wallet abroad (such as \uc2 PayPal\uc2 ) and receive dollar fees there.Convert dollars to a stable cryptocurrency into a "digital exchange house" from that same country, such as \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  in the United States.Sell cryptoactives in Argentina by pesos or dollars on a P2P platform or a local digital "exchange house". The problem with this option is that, according to specialists, it can bring legal problems.\par \par \uc2 The legality of the manoeuvre and its tax aspectHowever, what is the coexistence between this growing phenomenon and Argentine laws as to how a charge should be made to a customer abroad?\par \par \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Luis Ceteri, a specialist in tax issues, explained to Clar\u237\'00\'EDn that the reference regulations regulate exports of services. "Whether they are mono-contributors or in the general regime, freelancers have to make an E-export invoice when collecting their sales," he said.\par \par \uc2 He expanded: "That income is exempt from VAT and Gross Income. But the obtained \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be settled (sold by weights). And they must pay 5% withholding,unless the taxpayer is registered with the AFIP as a micro or small business, and provided that they have not exported more than $600 thousand the previous year."\par \par \uc2 According to Florencia Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Sabella, a tax specialist, today the problem with all this is that dollars earned abroad must "be liquidated than in pesos in Argentina through the Single Market and Free of Exchange (MULC), at the official exchange rate". That is, at less than $93 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Now, what if what was charged outside was not strictly dollars, but cryptocurrencies that equal dollars? There things are no longer so clear because the legislation was not done foreseting something like that.\par \par \uc2 According to Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Sabella, who is a partner of the Lai\u250\'00\'FAn studio, Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Sabella & Smudt, what is clearly forbidden is to collect dollars and, instead of liquidating them, convert them to cryptocurrency and then pass them to pesos. "That violates \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations and, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  detects it, it can make a complaint,"he said.\par \par \uc2 So, as Clar\u237\'00\'EDn may have known,what lawyers suggest to freelancers is that, instead of collecting in dollars, they charge in cryptocurrencies and pass them to pesos. The receipt or payment order must state that the payment was in crypto.\par \par \uc2 They recommend this because, well, the taxpayer can argue that he never received dollars? or euros or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that should be settled in the MULC at official value.\par \par \uc2 Collecting bitcoins or DAI would not require such liquidation because, even if those cryptoactives are exchanged on the market as if they were \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they are not yet considered as such by the rules.\par \par \uc2 Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Sabella explained that, then, in that case, another possible problem will be that the equal invoice must be made in dollars and that, for tax purposes, such value is converted to pesos at the official value.\par \par \uc2 So, for a sale of US$ 1,000 you can get $145 thousand to spend, but the AFIP will count that that only generated a consumption capacity of $91 thousand. Which, over time, can mean a freelancer to be excluded from the monotribute.\par \par \uc2 According to Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Sabella, since 2018, this difference can be understood as a profit obtained by the "buying and selling digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\u160\'00\'A0And AFIP could charge 15% on the difference between the official change and that of the "crypto dollar".\par \par \uc2 Mg\par \par \uc2 Look too\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210419eh4j00316\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
