\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina's real lender of last instance, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\b0\par\par\uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Alfredo Romano There were many episodes with the Fund over the past 65 years. However, I will focus on the last few decades, starting in 2001, where the \uc2 IMF\uc2  turned out to be a lender of last instance, as well as the Macri financial crisis of 2018. Argentina, having no stable, reliable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and maintaining its value over time, has also not had a lender of last instance since the role of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during both crises was absolutely limited and could not avoid the shock in the real economy.\par \par \uc2 A BCRA I water\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest problems our BCRA has in making monetary policy is that the depth of the financial sector in Argentina is very limited. Our country, besides that it has no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also does not have a deep financial system. What does it mean that the financial system is not deep? That less than 50% of Argentines have a bank account and that access to credit is extremely limited, where for example the credit to the first home represents 1.5% of gross domestic product (GDP) while in Chile it is around 20%. That is, in Argentina there is a very high level of reporting and the financial sector has no capillary in all the industries and productive sectors of the country. As a comparison, Chile has 80% of its banking economy. But what are the consequences of this on the monetary policy that the BCRA has? One of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest monetary policy tools is to manage the interest rate. The interest rate in an economy mainly regulates the liquidity of the system. A practical example is that when the interest rate rises people prefer to leave their savings on fixed deadlines rather than using those resources for consumption. That is, in addition to the effect it has on the circulation of pesos on the economy, it also has an effect on the real economy. Argentina's problem is that because it has a very small financial system, our BCRA does not have a direct transmission mechanism when it uses the interest rate as a monetary tool. To translate this technicality, the BCRA is a rengo Bank, unable to use its tools to control financial stress situations in the country. A good recent example is of the Argentina crisis in 2019, where the BCRA's interest rate was 78%, if 78% a year, but Argentines ran to the dollar because they preferred to buy dollars rather than make a return of more than 6% per month in pesos from their investments.\par \par \uc2 The concept to be carried away is that technically the BCRA does not have powerful tools available to restore stability in situations of financial volatility. So why do we have the BCRA if it generates inflation, the fall in real wages constantly, it is not able to reduce volatility in situations of financial stress and has also been powerless in financing us when we needed it most? I don't know either!\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, returning to the role of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , it is important to note that during the presidency of De la R\u250\'00\'FAa, the Fund takes on a leading role for Argentina, being one of the actors in the management of the financial crisis that the country faced. In exchange for further disbursement, it gradually increased its capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's definition of economic policy. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , with its acronym in MFI, is also recognized worldwide with a particular slogan "Its Mostly Fiscal" referencing that the main economic policy resource that this body uses as a remedy for crises is associated with fiscal adjustment.\par \par \uc2 In Argentina there is much rejection of the Fund and the policies it has carried out, which I agree have been technical recommendations in recent decades but with very serious conceptual errors. For example, in the 2018 crisis, it proved to be a lender of last instance when Argentina loses access to world credit and the Fund conditionals its disbursements on compliance with fiscal and monetary adjustment policies that deepened the recessionous impact of crises, particularly affecting the most vulnerable sectors. This similar scenario came during 2001, where Argentina suffered its worst economic crisis and \uc2 IMF\uc2  prescriptions mainly affected Argentina's most vulnerable social classes (Stiglitz, 2002).\par \par \uc2 So why couldn't the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  be our lender of last instance by avoiding the Fund's role in the latest deep economic crises? The answer is because we don't have \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and since we don't have \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  we have to borrow in dollars. By borrowing in dollars, our \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot issue green paper,it can only issue Argentine pesos. Every time the BCRA emits in situations of maximum economic stress to generate liquidity in the market or devalues to mitigate the economic shock, it has generated a contradictory effect. Instead of reducing volatility, generating peace of mind and regaining confidence, he has flaunted devaluatory flashes and deepened economic crises (Globalization and exchange regime: does it make sense to dollarize? Calvo, 1999).\par \par \uc2 It is a myth to hide in the loss of our BCRA as a lender of last instance to solve financial crises. Without going any further, again, we saw it in the pandemic crisis in 2020, where the BCRA has emitted a mountain of pesos to sustain economic activity, but the real economy fell 10% and inflation in 2020 was 36%. By 2021, the inflation estimate is around 50% due to higher monetary issuance. So, I ask you again, what's the point of having a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it doesn't give you stability and security in times of crisis and you also have to go to a multilateral agency to tell you what to do in exchange for financing?\par \par \uc2 In conclusion, many dollarization detractors argue that this monetary plan is effective in lowering inflation but that the cost associated with carrying it forward is greater than the long-term benefits it generates in the economy, mainly because monetary sovereignty and a lender of last instance are lost. Hard data show that BCRA's monetary sovereignty and role as a lender of last instance has been limited in recent Argentine crises.\par \par \uc2 (*) The author is director of Romano Group, director of the Capital Markets diploma at Universidad Austral and a master's degree in Finance and Public Policy (\uc2 Columbia University\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210415eh4f001on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
