\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades \uc2 YPF\uc2 's IDRs to 'RD' on Exchange Completion; Subsequently Upgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 '\b0\par\par\uc2 3,706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2021\par 18:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 18 Feb 2021 12:01 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades \uc2 YPF\uc2 's IDRs to 'RD' on Exchange Completion; Subsequently Upgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 '\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -New York-18 February 2021:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has downgraded \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2  .'s Long-Term Foreign and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) to 'RD' from 'C', due to the conclusion of its announced exchange offer. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has simultaneously upgraded \uc2 YPF\uc2  's Long-Term Local and Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' from 'RD', and upgraded the company's senior unsecured notes to 'CCC'/'RR4' from 'C'/'RR4'. The stand-alone credit profile was revised to 'b' from 'c'.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade and simultaneous upgrade reflect the completion of the announced exchange for its seven outstanding international bonds totaling $6.2 billion notes. Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's "Distressed Debt Exchange Criteria," the IDRs are downgraded to 'RD' upon completion and re-rated as the exchange resulted in a material reduction in the original terms of the bonds, evidenced by the extension of maturities and discounted coupon payment for two-years from issuance of the new notes.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 11, 2021, \uc2 YPF\uc2  announced the results of the exchange offer. \uc2 YPF\uc2  received 59.79% acceptance to exchange the outstanding March 2021 notes for the new senior secured 2026 notes, which are secured by a reserve and payment account, and with 50% of \uc2 YPF\uc2  's stake in YPF Luz pledged as collateral. Hold-out bond holders received the cash payment for the outstanding 40.21%, which was approved by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina\uc2  and has been settled. \uc2 YPF\uc2  also announced that it received 43.12% exchange for its April 2024 notes, 37.15% for its March 2025, 24.55% for its July 2025, 19.07% for its July 2027, 20.21% for its June 2029, and 28.45% for its 2047.\par \par \uc2 Collectively when including the 2021 exchange offer, \uc2 YPF\uc2  successfully exchanged $2.0 billion of its $6.2 billion outstanding bonds. The $2.0 billion exchange was allocated among three bonds. First, $775 million for the senior secured 2026 notes, which will have a coupon of 4.0% for the first two years through December 2022, and 9% thereafter, with a quarterly amortization payment made equally beginning in 2023. Second, $748 million for the senior unsecured bond due 2029 was issued with a coupon of 2.5% for the first two years through December 2022, and 9% thereafter with equal semi-annual amortization payments beginning in 2026. Third, $576 million for the senior unsecured bond due 2033 with a coupon of 1.5% through 2022 and 7% thereafter with annual amortization beginning 2029.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates the exchange offer will save \uc2 YPF\uc2  approximately $105 million in interest expense annually through the end of 2022, which \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects the company will allocate to upstream capex to ramp up production, increasing production by up to 40,000boed, when assuming that unconventional wells average cost is $5.5 million in Vaca Muerta and each new well produces on average 1,000boed. After 2022, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates the company's annual interest from its international bonds from 2023 through 2025 will be approximately $50 million higher before the exchange offer. On a consolidated basis, \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2  's annual interest expense will average $700 million through 2023.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  ratings are in line with \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's "Government Related Entities (GRE) Criteria." \uc2 YPF\uc2  is majority owned by the government and strategically important to the country. \uc2 YPF\uc2  's dominant market share in the supply of liquid fuels in Argentina, coupled with its large hydrocarbon production footprint in the country, could expose the company to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through pricing policies or investment strategies. \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  exerts strong control over \uc2 YPF\uc2  through energy regulations and/or its 51% economic interest in the company. Furthermore, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes Argentina would have strong incentives to support the company under a distress situation as a result of the strong socio-political and financial implications of a default.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Links to Sovereign: \uc2 YPF\uc2  is closely linked to the Republic of Argentina due to its ownership structure, as well as recent government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls the company through its 51% stake, and provincial government officials serve on the company's board of directors. In addition, the republic sometimes governs the company's strategy and business decisions. The Argentine government has a history of significant interference in the oil and gas sector. For example, via Decree No. 1277, the government set regulations related to investment levels in the oil and gas sector and domestic price reference points.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the government issued Decree 566/19, which negatively affected \uc2 YPF\uc2  's cash flows. Although \uc2 YPF\uc2  is a leading energy company in Argentina, government policies continue to present challenges, inhibiting its business strategy.\par \par \uc2 Moderate Leverage: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes \uc2 YPF\uc2  has a moderate leverage profile mostly due to a high total debt/1P and weak EBITDA/interest expense ratio in 2020, which is expected to improve over the rated horizon. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2  's total debt/EBITDA will be 3.6x in 2020 and improve to an average of 2.3x over the rated horizon; however, \uc2 YPF\uc2  's total debt/1P is high, estimated to be USD8.00 per boe, and consistent with the 'b' category. Furthermore, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2  's EBITDA to interest expense will be 2.6x in 2020 and improve to an average 4.0x thereafter.\par \par \uc2 High Production Cost Profile: \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating case assumes production will remain flat averaging 515,000boed over the rated horizon. \uc2 YPF\uc2  's cost of production is slightly lower than the guaranteed price under Barril Criollo between May and mid-August 2020 and \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's assumed Brent price for 2021 of USD48.00boe. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2  's half-cycle costs of USD26.5boe and full-cycle cost of USD41.5boe, which are both high and above average for players in the region.\par \par \uc2 The company's high cost is mostly attributed to higher than average lifting cost of USD13.5boe and high interest cost per barrel of USD5.0boe in 2019, estimated by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 . The company's full-cycle break-even implied prices were above weighted average realization prices for oil and gas, primarily as a result of decreasing domestic gas prices and high level of gas production, which accounts for approximately 49% of total production during 2019 (Crude oil production comprised 44% and natural gas liquids 7%).\par \par \uc2 Volatile Operating Environment: The volatile economic environment in Argentina has inhibited \uc2 YPF\uc2  from implementing its business strategy, i.e. unconventional development in Vaca Muerta. Pandemic-related quarantine measures have further stressed the company, as demand for fuels has decreased, gasoline volumes dropped by 70% and diesel by 34%, jet fuel 95%, with a total demand decrease of 50% in April 2020, compared with the previous year. Volumes appear to have recovered but remain below historical averages.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates Argentina's real GDP will contract by 11.2% in 2020, after negative average growth rate over the last three years. Inflation is expected to average 47% between 2020 and 2022, and government debt/GDP ratio is estimated to be 102% in 2020 and 105% in 2021, with a majority of government debt being external 75%-80% over the same time frame.\par \par \uc2 Derivation Summary\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  's linkage to the sovereign is similar in nature to its Latin American national oil companies (NOCs) peers, namely \uc2 PEMEX\uc2  (BB-/Stable), \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (BB-/Negative) and \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  (BBB-/Negative), and government-owned entities ENAP (A-/Stable), and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  (BBB+/Negative). These companies all have strong linkage to their respective sovereigns given their strategic importance to each country and the potentially significant negative social and financial implication a default could have at a national level.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  's upstream business closest peers are \uc2 Pemex\uc2  , \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  and \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  . \uc2 YPF\uc2  's total production averaged 514,000boed, and the reserve life was 5.7 years, most comparable with \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  with a 2019 production of 725,000boed and a reserve life of 7.8 years, but less than \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  ' production of 2.6 million boed and a reserve life of 10 years and \uc2 Pemex\uc2  's production at 2.8 million boed and a reserve life of 9.5 years.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  has a strong capital structure reporting a gross leverage ratio defined as total debt/EBITDA of 2.4x in 2019 and total debt/1P of USD7.67 per boe compared with \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  at 1.1x in 2019 and USD5.80 total debt/1P, \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  at 2.3x and USD6.60 per boe and \uc2 Pemex\uc2  at 6.2x and USD14.70 per boe.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its peers ENAP, \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  , \uc2 Pemex\uc2  and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  , \uc2 YPF\uc2  is not the sole provider of refined fuels in Argentina. In 2019, the company had nearly a 60% market share. \uc2 YPF\uc2  is an integrated energy company, similar to \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  and \uc2 Pemex\uc2  , offering the company more financial flexibility, while ENAP is predominately a refining company that sells to marketers.\par \par \uc2 Historically, \uc2 YPF\uc2  has operated autonomously with periodic controls of fuel prices and crude, which are currently in effect. Similar to \uc2 Pemex\uc2  and \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  , \uc2 YPF\uc2  has administered an import-parity pricing policy, but is evidence of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Decree 466/19 and other price controls in 2018 to tame inflation, which is projected to be 50% in 2020. Until recently, \uc2 YPF\uc2  has had success in tightening the spread between import parity and local prices.\par \par \uc2 When compared with downstream-focused entities ENAP and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  , \uc2 YPF\uc2  has a lower total debt/EBITDA ratio of 2.4x in 2019 compared with ENAP at 6.7x and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  at 18.0x. \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  's elevated leverage is explained by its investment plan to increase capacity by 2021, while ENAP has maintained a higher leverage profile for an extended period of time, but the company is highly strategic for the Chilean governments, and thus it rating is aligned as a result.\par \par \uc2 Key Assumptions\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's Key Assumptions Within its Rating Case for the Issuer\par \par \uc2 --Average gross production of 515,000boe from 2020-2023;\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 YPF\uc2  will be able to increase domestic prices in pesos somewhat but not enough to fully reflect the impact from the recent peso depreciation and international hydrocarbon price increase;\par \par \uc2 18 Feb 2021 12:01 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades \uc2 YPF\uc2 's IDRs to -2-\par \par \uc2 --Criollo barrel of USD45/bbl in place from May through Mid-August2020 and applied \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's price deck thereafter;\par \par \uc2 --Natural gas prices decrease to USD2.70/MMBTU in 2020, USD3.00/MMBTU in 2021, USD3.25/MMBTU in 2022 and USD3.57/MMBTU in 2023;\par \par \uc2 --Capex cut to be FCF positive over the rated horizon due to refinancing risk;\par \par \uc2 --Downstream sales volume follow Real GDP forecasts;\par \par \uc2 --USD111 million of net proceeds associated with the sale of 11% stake in Bandurria Sur to \uc2 Equinor\uc2  and \uc2 Shell\uc2  ; and a 50% stake in offshore area CAN_100 to \uc2 Equinor\uc2  in 2Q20;\par \par \uc2 --Fitch ARS/USD forecasts for year average and end of period during 2020-2022;\par \par \uc2 --Reflects exchange offer with coupon adjustment period per bond;\par \par \uc2 --Annual average capex of $2.5 billion from 2021-2024.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 --The Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR is linked to the sovereign rating of Argentina and thus an upgrade can only occur if there is an upgrade of the country ceiling of Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 --Argentina's sovereign rating is currently at the lowest level (\uc2 CCC\uc2 ) allowed under \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's sovereign criteria; therefore, a downgrade of Foreign and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR would reflect \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's belief that a default of some kind appears probable, or a default or default-like process has begun for the corporate, which will be represented by a 'CC' or 'C' rating.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Non-Financial Corporate issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Liquidity and Debt Structure\par \par \uc2 Adequate Liquidity: \uc2 YPF\uc2  reported USD1.3 billion in cash and cash equivalents in 3Q20. Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's estimates, this covers roughly two years of interest expense. The company's debt maturity profile did improve after the completion of its exchange particularly the exchange of its 2021 notes to 2026. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects \uc2 YPF\uc2  will continue to roll-over short term bank and trade financing debt over the rated horizon, and its first international bond maturity is in April 2024.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure due to Argentina federal government's majority ownership in \uc2 YPF\uc2  , which has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the rating in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2  .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Downgrade; RD\par \par \uc2 ; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Upgrade; \uc2 CCC\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Downgrade; RD\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Upgrade; CCC\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Upgrade; \uc2 CCC\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Saverio Minervini,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +1 212 908 0364\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings, Inc\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Hearst Tower 300 W. 57th Street\par \par \uc2 New York, NY 10019\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Jose Ramon Rio,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +56 2 2499 3316\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Lucas Aristizabal,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +1 312 368 3260\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Elizabeth Fogerty, New York, Tel: +1 212 908 0526, Email: elizabeth.fogerty@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Applicable Model\par \par \uc2 Numbers in parentheses accompanying applicable model(s) contain hyperlinks to criteria providing description of model(s).\par \par \uc2 Corporate Monitoring & Forecasting Model (COMFORT Model), v7.9.0 ( 1 )\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank R