\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Several companies had already renegotiating their debts before the Central\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to partially deny it access to the foreign market to pay off debt to companies fell like a bomb, the situation may be less dramatic than it looks at first glance. The amount of maturities that should be restructured amounts to about $950 million until March next year, but there are already several frontline companies that ventured into successful renegotiations with their creditors.\par \par \uc2 In business circles they harshly criticized the measure. Firstly because it was never consulted, so it surprised the market. But also because one of the goals of sovereign debt renegotiation was to open up the market for new business issues. But after this decision everything gets complicated. To the already known risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now added this type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the BCRA.\par \par \uc2 The Negotiable Obligations of internationally traded companies suffered sharp declines, ranging from 7% to 9%. This is a logical reaction from investors, given the fact that no company will pay the maturities of its debts in a timely way. According to the formula defined by the BCRA, only 40% of what they must pay can access and the remaining 60% will have to refinance it to a minimum period of two years. In addition, they will be required to submit a plan to the institution before the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 The Negotiable Obligations - debt securities - of internationally traded companies suffered sharp declines, ranging from 7% to 9%.\par \par \uc2 Companies and banks with upcoming maturities recognize that they are unwilling to use their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet the maturity of their bonds. They should go through the "liquidation count" market and admit an exchange rate that is no longer $75 but $130. Sincere these values would mean recognizing a millionaire red who would leave the company on the brink of bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 A witness case would be that of \uc2 Banco Hipotecario\uc2 , which has an end-november maturity of nearly $300 million. From the entity they have already anticipated that they will move forward with the "reprofileling", which will require trading with their bondholders.\par \par \uc2 Telecom got 77.7% of its creditors to agree to stretch maturities falling in 2021For Central, corporate debt maturities represent one of the reserve outing channels. The average was around $500 million per month, so it's actually a smaller amount relative to demand for the "solidarity" dollar (in August it was more than $1 billion). But this is an unreleased measure, by getting into companies' relationship with their creditors.\par \par \uc2 In recent months several companies managed to renegotiate their debts, in the face of strong maturities that would have been impossible to refinance under current market conditions. One case was \uc2 YPF\uc2  itself, which had a maturity of $1 billion in 2021. Before approaching the total default, the state-majority oil company achieved 58% adhesion to stretch repayments by 2025. Of course, the remaining 42%, equivalent to about $450 million, will have to enter a new restructuring. There was no capital outflow, but lengthening deadlines and even a higher interest coupon.\par \par \uc2 Central's decision to restrict companies from accessing dollars to pay off debt fell like a bomb on the market and corporate ONes lost between 7% and 9%. But companies recognize that it is feasible to move towards a reprofileling friendly to60 per cent of their maturities.\par \par \uc2 Something similar happened with Telecom, who got 77.7% of his creditors to agree to stretch the maturities that fell in 2021. The telecommunications company also stretched its debt, as did \uc2 YPF\uc2 , until 2025. Aeropuertos Argentina 2000 managed to refinance dollar debt with banks until 2027, amounting to more than US$120 million. Another who got a friendly renegotiation was real estate developer Raghsa.\par \par \uc2 At central they claim that they took into account these transactions, which achieved a relatively high percentage of membership. That's why he agrees to hand over dollars for 40% and the rest should be restructured. Of course, if companies fail to negotiate, they would be doomed to a default.\par \par \uc2 The list of companies that must renegotiate their debts also includes IRSA, \uc2 Cresud\uc2 , \uc2 Genneia\uc2 , and Petroagro, among the most relevant. There are also many with maturities less than $5 or $6 million, but in this case there would be no major problems. The regulations allow you to pay at a rate of USD 1 million per month without authorization.\par \par \uc2 "We need to stretch the maturities for the next six months until the coarse harvest dollars begin to enter in 2021. Let's hope that by that time we can return to some normality," they explain at the BCRA.\par \par \uc2 I kept reading:\par \par \uc2 The reason behind the harsh exchange rate measures: liquid reserves are just $2.8 billion\par \par \uc2 For the field, new exchange rate restrictions deepen lack of confidence and affect investment and employment\par \par \uc2 Since last year's PASO, Argentine wall street companies have lost up to 83% of their value\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200917eg9h002xv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
