\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Argentine Banks Increased Public-Sector Exposure a Credit Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 1,950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2020\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 9 Sep 2020 16:00 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Argentine Banks Increased Public-Sector Exposure a Credit Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 -New York/Santiago-09 September 2020: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulations by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BCRA), such as capping interest rates offered on loans, placing floors for deposit rates amid very low credit growth and forbearance programs that exclude bank holdings of newly issued sovereign debt from regulatory limits for public-sector debt exposures, have increased Argentine banks' exposure to the public sector, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2   says. These developments are viewed negatively from a credit perspective, given the government's weak financial profile and the banks' increasing concentration risk as a result of these measures. Amid a backdrop of inflation adjustments enacted this year that eroded bank profitability, interest rate caps and floors further reduced Argentine banks' profitability. Banks have been forced to invest their excess liquidity in public sector assets, mainly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  securities and other sovereign bonds, given the lack of alternative profitable options. At current low levels, public sector assets do not affect the banks' ratings (IDRs of 'CC'); however, this exposure could become more of a constraint over the medium term as the sovereign is lifted out of default. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects banks' public-sector holdings to decline gradually when private sector lending resumes, although this will be heavily dependent on the path of the economic recovery. However, some banks only invest in BCRA assets as they have significantly less risk than sovereign bonds, although some asset classes are less profitable. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2  believes this strategy is much more conservative and supportive of credit. To compensate for interest rate controls, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been reducing the legal reserve requirement (encaje) and expanding the limit for Leliq (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  short-term securities), which pay a 38% interest rate. When banks take deposits at 33%, part of the funds held to meet the legal reserve requirement are placed in Leliqs at 38%. The rest are either lent to the private sector, invested in REPOS with the BCRA that pay 19%, or are invested in more risky options (like sovereign bonds linked to inflation). Nearly the only type of private-sector credit currently growing in Argentina is under the credit facility for small and medium-sized enterprises established by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which carries an interest rate of 24% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent agreement for the sovereign debt restructuring is good news for the financial system but is only the first step of many that the government has to take to reduce the huge macroeconomic imbalances and revive the economy. The agreement is positive, as it will likely help to normalize relations with the international financial community. It should also boost investor, business and hopefully consumer confidence, which will contribute to economic recovery and benefit the banking system. The agreement also opens the door for international debt issuances for the private sector. This will also provide more flexibility for those banks that need to refinance maturing debt or access longer term funding at lower interest rates, although most banks do not need external financing due to current liquidity levels. Nevertheless, risks to Argentina's operating environment amid the pandemic fallout are clearly skewed to the downside, which led to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2 's revision of its outlook on the operating environment and asset quality scores to negative from stable for these banks. Market volatility, a sustained contraction in real terms of loans, rising nonperforming loans, higher credit costs and rising administrative expenses due to high inflation will further exacerbate financial profile risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contact: Santiago Gallo Director, Latin American Financial Institutions +56 2 2499 3320 Fitch Chile Clasificadora de Riesgo Ltda. Alcantara 200, Of. 202, Las Condes Santiago, Chile Theresa Paiz Fredel Senior Director, Latin American Financial Institutions Financial Institutions +1 212 908-0534 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  . 33 Whitehall Street New York, NY 10004 Laura Kaster, CFA Senior Director, Fitch Wire Financials, North and South America +1 646 582-4497\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Media Relations: Elizabeth Fogerty, New York, Tel: +1 212 908 0526, Email: elizabeth.fogerty@thefitchgroup.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . All opinions expressed are those of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: HTTPS://WWW.FITCH