\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Result of staticism: in Argentina most children are poor\b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Total direct relationship: in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  as titism and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy grows, the poor are multiplying. When are we going to start discussing the problem? (\uc2 Twitter\uc2 )Argentina has been trying to put out a fire for years by pouring fuel. We're kind of drunks we take to forget we have an alcohol problem. While all the facts and empirical evidence point the right way, we still want to end the drama of poverty with more state, more regulation of the economy, and more taxes so that we can one day "reach the redistribution of wealth equally."\par \par \uc2 Our story is clear. The fraticide desert that took place from our Independence to the fall of Roses was practically no man's land. The ideas of progress embodied in the Constitution of Juan Bautista Alberdi made the country the richest in the world by 1895. We didn't learn anything anyway. The popular journalist Chiche Gelblung, in one hand with the liberal journalist Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Zald\u237\'00\'EDvar, criticized him this week that in the period of Argentina his grandparents "starved to death" and had to make "soup with a potato".\par \par \uc2 What Gelblung should answer is this: Before his grandparents reach that Argentina that seduced immigration from the world, did they eat more or less than that poor potato soup every day? When you left this world, and after living in that productive country, did your grandparents have more or less than what they possessed at birth? No one says that it was all a bed of roses or that the path of progress is easy to travel. But what is left with no doubt is that, in the face of the options available, the fruits of freedom are absolutely superseders to all the states experiments in history.\par \par \uc2 When the process of wealth creation does not stop, the long-awaited redistribution naturally arises spontaneously through the capitalization of the economy. Argentine socialists who continue to question and ridicule the idea of "the invisible hand" will soon see something inevitable: how countries they questioned for using "cheap labor" will have wages in real terms higher than ours. A couple more devaluations are missing, some new zero to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  courtesy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its ticket printer machine and ready... we're done.\par \par \uc2 Data published today on \uc2 unicef\uc2 's recent (\uc2 United Nations Children's Fund\uc2 ) projections are alarming. By the end of the year, child poverty at the national level would exceed 63%. According to these numbers, the number of poor children in Argentina would range from 7 million to 8.3. When we talk about extreme poverty, the rising numbers are also very worrying: Argentina would no longer have 16.3% of boys in that situation to move to 18.5%.\par \par \uc2 Unicef\uc2 's research is conducted with official data from the National Institute of Statistics and Census (INDEC) and uses the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's national GDP projection for these hours.\par \par \uc2 Although it sounds outrageous, in the face of this worrying scenario, the political class as a whole proposes to deepen the path that led us to this disaster. Eventually the reality will be undeniable and the country will have to go in another direction. The question is the bigger the problem we will have to fix when that moment happens.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200806eg850007b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
