\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine reserves drip out even as government tightens controls\b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2020\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, June 4 (Reuters) - Argentina's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are falling fast despite the government's tightening of access to dollars, hitting the lowest level since early 2017 in May as the country struggles to control the coronavirus and a long-simmering debt crisis.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell almost $1 billion last month as the entity looked to prop up Argentina's peso, and have tumbled around $3 billion since early January. They now stand at $42.6 billion, down from a peak near $80 billion early last year.\par \par \uc2 The low levels have raised a red flag as Argentina looks to hike emergency spending to fight the COVID-19 pandemic, dig out of a biting recession and appease foreign creditors pushing for a bigger payout in a $65 billion debt restructuring.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sought to restrict companies' access to dollars in recent weeks. Private citizens can only buy $200 in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the official exchange market per month, and must pay a steep tax.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the restrictions on buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... growing uncertainty has unleashed a new round of falling reserves," local consultancy Ecolatina said, adding the "alarming levels" could prompt more tightening.\par \par \uc2 The South American country imposed tough capital controls last year after a market crash led to a dramatic pick-up in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked to prop up a weakened peso and savers shifted to safe-haven dollars.\par \par \uc2 Argentine officials say they want to relax controls once the economy has been stabilized and a debt deal reached. But fears remain about a damaging run on the peso in a country that has struggled to build confidence in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current international reserves amount to less than the total value of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's imports last year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have helped keep the peso mostly stable recently, but its value on unofficial markets has plummeted, creating a worrying gap with the official rate.\par \par \uc2 The government is inching closer to a vital debt restructuring, but still faces tough hurdles to reach a comprehensive deal with creditors.\par \par \uc2 "While the negotiations last, we expect strong control over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain in place given the backdrop of falling reserves and high demand for dollars," said Argentine financial advisory Portfolio Personal Inversiones.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Walter Bianchi Writing by Adam Jourdan; Editing by Tom Brown)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-6-7T10:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200604eg6401of1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
