\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  regroups: abandons license to trade on the Porte\u241\'00\'F1a Stock Exchange\b0\par\par\uc2 1,180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Carlos Burgue\u241\'00\'F1ocburgueno@ambito.com.arThe very low level of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Argentine capital market that has declined since the 2018 crisis; the little certainty that this can be recovered in the medium term and some distrust of what an eventual next opposition-toned government (especially to financial business in Argentina in general) could cause one of the most waged houses by the government of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  a few days ago he made a final decision. \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  returned on 2 October (the news became known in recent days) its license to operate on the local market, through a letter sent to the National Securities Commission (CNV) where the international bank deregistered as a stockbroker. While it was a house of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was hardly in use, the resignation of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the delivery of the license somehow mark an end of time.\par \par \uc2  The license had been opened in early 2016 to operate in what was expected to be an expansive capital market and growing in both volume and widespread profits. A spiralization of local and global interest in betting on offers that would theoretically multiply before a country that, hand in hand with the new government, would return to capitalism after the populist experience, especially after the last four years of management of \uc2 Cristina Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez de Kirchner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  not only opened a representation on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange (it had withdrawn in 2002, after the crisis of convertibility), but also bet big on the success of Christianity. The Us entity had been the workplace of several of the most important officials of the Macrism. Among others they had passed through their Offices of Wall Street and the rest of the world Alfonso Prat Gay, Luis "Toto" Caputo and Vladimir Werning,all men of maximum responsibility in different stages of the first three years of government of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 ,who also maintained management relationships with the bank during his task in the civil service. It had also been a key strategic ally in the first two years of Change management. It had opened an office in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-2015 and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Government of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 : the placement of the Bonds of the Argentine Republic at the beginning of the management Let's change to pay the holdouts. In total, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  had participated in voluntary debt placements of about US$2.3 billion in Macri's first year in power, outperforming other houses such as \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , Santander, \uc2 BBVA\uc2  and Deustche Bank and HSBC, all entities that, with more or more or more less presence, they had always operated in the country and had never left the local square, even in the times of default and Kirchnerism. The good relationship continued (and intensified) throughout 2017 and until March 2018 \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  recommended to its customers in its reports to bet on Argentina. Even in a job given to the bank's management slums around the world in the first week of April 2018 (already with the novelty of the consolidated Fed's interest rate hike), it recommended the long-term peso positions issued by the Argentine government and the Bank centrico. Including the Lebac.\par \par \uc2 However, on April 25, 2018 the romance ended. On that day they began to reach the capital market, and violently (at any exit price) orders to buy positions in Lebac from "big hands", which by definition, can decide, for better or worse, the fate of an entire period of \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The rumor thatcirculated that day in the international banks that operated Lebac the most (both American and European) was that the sellers were few. No more than three. And that the intention was to part with all the holding in that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  title and buy dollars quickly, closing the "carry trade"circuit. The price offered at the beginning of the day was $20.50, almost a peso higher than the quote just a day earlier, thus the conviction of the orders created even more slobs in Argentine traders. The biggest surprise came when it became known, almost by an oversight of one of the bond sellers to one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, which at the center of sales was the US bank \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . On that day, it began to detach from the most symbolic titles of Macrism: the Lebac of the BCRA,the instrument by which Argentina's exchange rate stability was also maintained.The April 25 financial chronicle described a "perfect storm." All the ghosts numbed on some side of the screens of the country's major operators in the country and the world coincided in a single day: the March rate rise in the United States, the start of the trade crisis with China, the local ghost of the indomitable inflation and its battle against interest rates in pesos, end-of-month payments to importers, delay in the settlement of the sogera, the beginning of the application of the financial income tax and its influence on the profits of the Lebac , the eternal debate within the Macrism over the rise of tariffs, the threat of further spikes in fuels and the eventual lack of dollars from drought. Analysts were already publicly questioning the effects of that failed 28-D press conference (2017) at which Marcos Pe\u241\'00\'F1a live and live took away the BCRA in view of the whole country's responsibility to control the monetary base and set the goals for to address the most important problem in the Argentine economy: inflation. Since that post-election day, Pe\u241\'00\'F1a took personal control of the official management of the Argentine economy, a decision that had not yet been digested by the markets, and which raised doubts about the true official conviction of placing the fight against the inflation in the maximum ranking of official priorities.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's call to part with the Lebac, buy dollars and leave the Argentine market generated widespread fear in the government. To such an extent that the decision to accept the transaction was consulted with \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  himself, who authorized the departure of the BCRA dollars to supply the demand of the American bank. Immediately after the sale of dollars to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  was endorsed, two other major trades completed the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales outflows out of BCRA's reserves for $1.472 million. A record number, which with weeks would be surpassed. A few weeks after that April 25, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  himself was distributing a lapidary report to his VIP clients. In it he considered whether the tool that the BCRA had implemented to deal with the run (the interest rate rise) could be insufficient antidote to the obvious capital flight that had been initiated and revised to the worse all its forecasts for the economy Argentina in 2018: 2.4% growth and 22% inflation. It also advanced a dollar to 24 pesos as of December. Given what happened at the end of 2018, the advances were even children.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191022efam000uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
