\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 7-Wall Street traders pump up Argentine peso as  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  controls hit home\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2019\par 23:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Fitch upgrade, updates other prices)\par \par \uc2 By Cassandra Garrison and Jorge Otaola\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso surged on Tuesday, pumped up by Wall Street traders cheering President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's capital controls that are aimed at protecting the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso closed 5.39% higher at 55.98 per U.S. dollar, traders said, its strongest level in a week after a near record low close on Friday. Country risk, which soared to its highest level in more than a decade last month, also fell.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso in the afternoon by selling dollars from its reserves. The bank, which declined to comment, has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, selling nearly a billion dollars in the second half of last week alone.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound gives some relief to Macri, after a shock primary election battering last month sparked a sharp crash in the country's bonds, equities and the peso, which lost around 26% of its value against the greenback in August alone.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's S&P Merval stock index, however, took a hit in afternoon trading, closing down 11.9% at a two-year low in a slide that local analysts attributed to global trade jitters.\par \par \uc2 "All the markets are in the red and a weakened country like Argentina, with a complicated political situation, gets hits even more," said Hector Scasserra, director of local brokerage firm Arpenta Sociedad de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the pounding of Argentina's bonds also eased after they had fallen to record lows on Monday, while there were tentative gains in some foreign-traded bank shares.\par \par \uc2 Ratings agency Fitch upgraded Argentina's long-term issuer debt rating to "CC" or just barely above default from "restricted default" saying the new rating reflects their view that "a further default or debt restructuring of some kind is probable."\par \par \uc2 Frankfurt-listed American Depositary Receipts (ADRs) of \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia\uc2  were up 0.6% after having tumbled 7.7.% on Monday while \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2 's ADRs fell over 6% in New York, where markets were closed on Monday for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 MarketAxess data showed Argentina's century bond traded marginally higher while the January 2028 bond dipped under 37 cents on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The black market peso, available via unofficial traders, also rose on Tuesday, although not as sharply as the official spot rate. Following a fall on Monday, it has now diverged from the official rate by the largest margin since 2015, a sign of tension over pricing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STILL AN EMERGING MARKET\par \par \uc2 Index provider MSCI said it was closely monitoring developments in Argentina but that the country's "emerging market" status for the purpose of MSCI indexes remained valid for now as MSCI Argentina only held foreign-listed shares.\par \par \uc2 Argentina was included in the emerging markets index in May, potentially boosting inflows to the eight stocks that were added.\par \par \uc2 Any reclassification of Argentina in the widely followed indexes would first require a public consultation from MSCI.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the government authorized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrict purchases of dollars as it burns through its reserves to prop up the peso. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls were a 180-degree turn for Macri, a free-markets advocate who abolished capital controls after he came to power in 2015.\par \par \uc2 It was the government's latest attempt to stabilize the peso, which has lost around 20% of its value since opposition presidential candidate Alberto Fernandez emerged as the clear front-runner in the Aug. 11 primary election.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the government also said in a decree published in the country's official gazette that it would unilaterally hike the minimum wage by 35% over the next few months. Argentina's 12-month inflation is running at close to 55%.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez and his running mate, former President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner\uc2 , are considered a riskier prospect by investors, who fear \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  could return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of her former government.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  called Argentina's financial system "strong" and said the bank would adhere to its strict monetary policy, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Sandleris, speaking at a news conference, said the bank was in talks with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to "redefine" the monetary goals for September under its $57 billion financing agreement. (Reporting by Marc Jones in London; Cassandra Garrison, Eliana Raszewski and Jorge Otaola in Buenos Aires and Rodrigo Campos in New York; Writing by Adam Jourdan; Editing by Rosalba O'Brien, Steve Orlofsky and Sandra Maler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-9-3T22:47:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190903ef93028bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
