\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's peso under scrutiny following capital controls, as U.S. markets reopen\b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2019\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON/BUENOS AIRES, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Investors are bracing to see how Argentine assets fare on Tuesday as U.S. markets reopen after a holiday weekend and Latin America's third largest economy marks its second day under new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls.\par \par \uc2 The pounding of Argentina's bonds eased in Europe after they fell to record lows on Monday and there were tentative gains in some foreign-traded bank shares, as investors waited for Wall Street's first reaction to Buenos Aires' imposition of capital controls over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Frankfurt-listed American Depositary Receipts (ADR) of \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia\uc2  were up almost 1%, having tumbled 9.15% on Monday, but \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2 's ADRs slipped again. Brokers were quoting marginally higher prices for Argentina's badly mauled sovereign debt in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Investors were cautiously awaiting the start of U.S. trading and for Wall Street's reaction to the weekend developments as U.S. markets were closed for a public holiday on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed 0.88% stronger in official markets on Monday, but ended 0.79% weaker in the black market at 63.5 per dollar, a divergence underscoring mistrust of the official price.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the government authorized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrict purchases of dollars as it burns through its reserves to prop up the peso. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls were a 180-degree turn for President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 , a free-markets advocate who abolished capital controls after he came to power in 2015.\par \par \uc2 It was the government's latest attempt to stabilize the peso, which has lost 28% of its value since opposition presidential candidate Alberto Fernandez emerged as the clear front-runner in the Aug. 11 primary election.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez and his running mate, former President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner\uc2 , are considered a riskier prospect by investors, who fear \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  could return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of her former government.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  called Argentina's financial system "strong" and said the bank would adhere to its strict monetary policy, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Sandleris, speaking at a press conference, said the bank was in talks with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to "redefine" the goals for September under its $57 billion financing agreement.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Marc Jones in London and Cassandra Garrison in Buenos Aires) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-9-3T13:21:44.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190903ef9301zol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
