\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina's dollar fever\b0\par\par\uc2 1,217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The problems generated by the dollar in Argentina became part of the permanent agenda of the whole society. The current management took the issue seriously and tried incessantly to reduce the fiscal deficit, contract the monetary issue and increase international reserves.\par \par \uc2 Each of these issues was addressed consistently, achieving considerable changes that, by the way, are quantitatively measurable. Since August 2018, the Government has reduced the money issue to virtually zero, narrowed the primary deficit by 2.5%, and doubled international reserves via \uc2 IMF\uc2  borrowing.\par \par \uc2 However, and despite this, the dollar in Argentina went from $20 in April 2018 to $60 per dollar in August 2019. A 200% change. How is this possible? How do you explain such a slump after so much effort?\par \par \uc2 The dollar went from $20 in April 2018 to $60 in August 2019.\par \par \uc2 To measure the effectiveness of an action, it is always advisable to measure it comparatively with a similar one. One case is the exit from convertibility, where the dollar went from being worth $1 in December 2001, to $2.8 per dollar in 2003, (180%). In 2002 the worst crisis in Argentine economic history was experienced, however the devaluation (considering the context) was even more benign than the current one.\par \par \uc2 Another valid comparison is that which occurred in Argentina during the second largest global crisis in history: "\uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ", in 2009. The devaluation at the time was 30%. In both cases, the shift government increased its fiscal deficit and expanded and relaxed the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the hands of the public.\par \par \uc2 But then, if the current government was more tax and monetary success, is there anything clearly identifiable that previous governments have done and the current one never did to prevent a cash run?\par \par \uc2 It is highly likely that this is happening in the field of exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Central banks in emerging countries have several ways of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market:there are ineffective, medium-effective and highly effective ways.\u160\'00\'A0The difference between the ineffective and the highly effective is that the former accompany a devaluation by softening it (what we see today), while the latter reverse the devaluation generating appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a market requires intelligence and a specific strategy to make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as effective as possible. The key is how the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells the reserves. Passive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies prevent the price from rising from a certain level, or ensure that the value in question does not rise so quickly, simply by accompanying it.\par \par \uc2 The other way of trading implies that when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it sells dollars at sequentially lower prices. This way of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is called active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies.\par \par \uc2 Our \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the last four years never used the active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism,always used the liability, supplying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the market, but increasingly, at ever higher prices, generating a scope of negotiation between the rest of buyers and sellers at ever-higher values. However, the most effective mechanism would have been to sell at lower prices.\par \par \uc2 But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually sold the dollar at lower prices, is it possible that there is more demand at those levels, and therefore would it lose its reserves more quickly?\par \par \uc2 Conversely than the rest of the products, in the case of the dollar, a higher price, rather than meaning lower demand(if the price of the tomato is higher, less tomato), implies an increase in appetite for ticket purchase. This happens because an x-price risather generates massive advertising via average, leading to increased demand for coverage from the public.In the case of the dollar, a higher price generates an automatic retraction of the offer.\par \par \uc2 According to the law of supply and demand, if the price of the tomato goes up, on the supply side (sell side) there will be more tomato producers eager to offer their merchandise.\u160\'00\'A0However in the case of the dollar, a higher price generates an automatic retraction of the offer,that is, who planned to sell dollars, in the face of the accelerated price rise, retracts and sells less or sells nothing. The combination of these two factors creates a pressure effect on trading, which spirals the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to completely unexpected levels, which in many cases are inconsistent with the fundamentals of the economy.\par \par \uc2 So are we saying that, in certain situations, the dollar rise could be a possible bubble? In certain situations, most likely. It is still difficult for this bubble to burst on its own, because product prices inflation, causing the dollar's ridiculously high price to cease to be so, and for other domestic prices to settle to the new level of the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not Vice versa.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, with an active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BCRA what is generated is a drop in the price that prevents the mass spread of the product and thus prevents greater demand. On the other hand, eventually by generating a considerable drop in the price (clicking the bubble), exporters/dollar sellers know that waiting for a higher price is pointless and thus avoiding the speculation that the drip sale produces. The conclusion, even when it seems crazy, is that at many times in time, lower dollar prices mean lower demand and higher exchange supply of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To synthesize, we will say that a considerable price increase, at a certain moment, awakens what we call "dollar-fever or dollar fever" in society.\u160\'00\'A0So avoiding abrupt climbs, it is possible to keep the virus asleep.\par \par \uc2 A change in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  behavior, leading to recurring, unexpired hikes, recreates widespread behavior that spirals demand for dollars and reduces the supply/sale of dollars to a minimum. An active dollar-fever virus.\par \par \uc2 In my 17 years participating in the exchange market I have seen the different efforts of the \uc2 Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I have also monitored the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Turkish lira and the Brazilian real. And in all cases, except in these last 4 years in our country, central banks when they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  do so with active policies,lowering the price of the dollar, or what is the same, appreciating the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , punishing with this those who bought expensive on the one hand, and hurting those who speculated waiting too long to sell at a higher price, on the other.\par \par \uc2 Passive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are apparently primarily responsible for the spiralization of the dollar price in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Passive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, it appear to be primarily responsible for the spiralization of the dollar price in Argentina, and each exchange rate increase of this kind, was responsible for a subsequent spiral in inflation that caused it to that high price of the dollar, become the new floor, and the conditions are prepared for a next round of feedback between dollar and domestic prices.\par \par \uc2 It is not rational that in a 12-month period, where international variableshad remained stable, local variables (deficit, issue and reserves) improved ostensibly, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed bullishly in the range of permanent historical crisis. After the result of the STEPS and debuting the default of the debt, you reach a fire scenario, with the house already incinerated and with very little water available. Let's hope it's not too late.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190902ef91003tt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
