\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 16-Argentina says to extend maturities of international bonds, \uc2 IMF\uc2  debt\b0\par\par\uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2019\par 02:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds bond market and political context)               \par \par \uc2 By Eliana Raszewski and Hugh Bronstein               \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 28 (Reuters) - Argentina will negotiate with holders of its sovereign bonds and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to extend the maturities of its debt obligations as a way of ensuring the country's ability to pay, Treasury Minister Hernan Lacunza said on Wednesday.               \par \par \uc2 At a news conference after meetings with an \uc2 IMF\uc2  team visiting Argentina, Lacunza said the government would "re-profile" the maturities of debt owed to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  under a $57 billion standby agreement.               \par \par \uc2 Interest and principal payments on bonds issued under international and local law will not be altered in the re-profiling. The changes in maturities would be aimed at obligations held by institutional, rather than individual investors, he said.               \par \par \uc2 "The priority today is to guarantee stability, because it is useless to launch reactivating measures if there is no stability. The first thing is to recover that stability," Lacunza said at the news conference in Buenos Aires.               \par \par \uc2 The peso took a beating during the day, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for a second consecutive day.               \par \par \uc2 Argentine asset prices have gotten slammed since the Aug. 11 primary election showed business-friendly President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  has surprisingly little public support in his campaign to win a second term in the October general election. He was trounced in the primary by center-left Peronist challenger Alberto Fernandez, who is now the clear front-runner.               \par \par \uc2 "President Macri instructed me to solve the short-term problem to guarantee electoral stability, but also in the medium- and long-term so as not to leave a problem for the person who follows, be it he or another candidate," Lacunza said.               \par \par \uc2 He said the measures were taken "so that the president can deploy his policies without the restriction of imminent, or too high, debt maturities."               \par \par \uc2 Argentina's peso closed 3.1% weaker at 58.1 per dollar on Wednesday, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $367 million of its reserves in a second consecutive day of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at controlling the peso's fall.               \par \par \uc2 Worries over \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to meet its dollar-denominated debt obligations have increased since the Aug. 11 primary. The peso has lost almost 22% of its value against the U.S. dollar since then. The weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has inflamed market concerns about Argentina's ability to pay its dollar-denominated obligations.               \par \par \uc2 Changes in the maturities of short-term debt, known as Letes and Lecap, will be between three and six months, Lacunza said.               \par \par \uc2 He said changes in maturities of bonds issued under Argentine law would require approval from Congress.               \par \par \uc2 "The markets will see this as a default," Hernan Esteves, economist with Buenos Aires consultancy FyEConsult, told Reuters.               \par \par \uc2 "The problem is that you have a debt restructuring proposal launched by one government, but that will have to be executed by the next government," Esteves said. "That makes it very difficult to manage an orderly restructuring."               \par \par \uc2 During trading hours on Wednesday, both Argentina's century bond and the January 2028 traded near 42.5 cents on the dollar, their lowest on record according to MarketAxess data.                              \par \par \uc2 "RECOGNIZING REALITY"               \par \par \uc2 "I don't like to use the word default. I prefer to think of it as recognizing reality," said Roger Horn, executive director and senior emerging market strategist at SMBC Nikko Securities America in New York. "With bonds trading in the 40s (cents on the dollar) the market was already expecting a worst-case scenario. It's better to deal with it now than six months from now."               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said in a statement that it was analyzing the government's new debt plan. Argentina has taken "important steps" to address liquidity needs and safeguard reserves, said the statement from IMF spokesman \uc2 Gerry Rice\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a statement saying it would continue to implement a "restrictive monetary policy" and continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to bolster the peso.               \par \par \uc2 The pushing out of debt maturities was aimed in part at preserving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar reserves, which stood at $57.9 billion as of Aug. 26.               \par \par \uc2 "The decisions that have been taken prioritize the use of international reserves to preserve monetary and financial stability, even if this implies delaying payments to large public debt investors," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  said in a separate statement.               \par \par \uc2 "These decisions should lessen the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, reduce the eventual demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and guarantee the availability of resources to limit volatility," it said.               \par \par \uc2 Macri took office in late 2015, promising to use orthodox economics to end the cyclical crises that have afflicted Argentina for decades.               \par \par \uc2 But he overestimated his ability to attract the foreign direct investment needed for sustainable growth, while under-estimating the effect that cutting public utility subsidies would have on inflation. His popularity fell as consumer prices, especially home heating gas and electricity bills, shot higher.               \par \par \uc2 The economy is currently in recession with 55% inflation.                                             \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-29T01:25:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190828ef8s02q87\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
