\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Will Continue  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2019\par 15:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0901 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  , says his office will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, adding that the country's financial market is "very solid" following a sharp depreciation of the peso last week. Sandleris says that the weaker peso is already having an impact in prices, and that inflation will rise in August and September. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 0855 ET - Argentina's new finance minister, \uc2 Hernan Lacunza\uc2 , says his priority will be to ensure stability for the peso after it depreciated sharply last week on investor concerns about the return to power of the leftist opposition. Lacunza says the peso was "above its level of equilibrium." Lacunza also says that inflation will likely accelerate this month after easing in July. He adds that the government is optimistic it can still meet its fiscal targets this year, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility and the new measures announced last week to help Argentines struggling with a recession and high inflation. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 0855 ET - The FTSE 100 rises as the stand-off between London and Brussels over the U.K.'s troubled plan to leave the EU knocks the pound. The blue-chip index gains 0.2%, or 12.24 points to 7201.89 as sterling falls 0.25% against the dollar and 0.28% against the euro. The EU flatly rejected Boris Johnson 's demand that it drops the Irish backstop from the withdrawal agreement reached with the UK. European Council President \uc2 Donald Tusk\uc2  reportedly said that by not proposing realistic alternative arrangements, those opposing the backstop "in fact support re-establishing a border" between Northern Ireland and the Irish Republic. Miner BHP Group drops 1.6% after full-year results. Home-builder \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  gains 0.6% as first-half margins modestly topped hopes. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 ET - Many U.K. gilt market makers and investors favored an index-linked gilt transaction in Q3 2019-20, the Debt Management Office says after Monday's consultation meetings with gilt market participants ahead of the announcement of the October-December 2019 gilt \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  calendar. Market makers advocated for the re-openings of index-linked gilts maturing in 2041 and 2048, as well as for the launch of a new bond in the 10- to 20-year maturity area, preferably in November. There were also a few calls for a long conventional gilt syndication, with others suggesting the choice over the type of bond be left open at this early stage. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0834 ET - August has been a difficult month for risky assets thus far, featuring investors' concerns about the possibility of a coming recession and leaving Unigestion's multi-asset team cautious. "While we think central banks will continue responding to the economic slowdown with more accommodation, thereby buoying markets, we have become more cautious in our exposures," it says. It sees markets focusing on sentiment which it says will remain challenging for many assets. Unigestion is thus "largely neutral" on equity markets, underweight credit and slightly overweight sovereign bonds. "Until we perceive that the market is ready to shift its focus from rather negative sentiment to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -backed positive macro momentum, we think it is best to remain cautious," it says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0826 ET - \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  has cut forecasts for several G10 European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, but it is positive on the safe-haven Swiss franc and upgrades its already-upbeat forecasts, citing late-cycle dynamics and escalating trade tension. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  now forecasts EUR/CHF will fall to 1.07 by year-end, compared with a previous forecast of 1.08; and to 1.06 in 2Q 2020, versus 1.07 previously. EUR/CHF is last down 0.1% at 1.0861. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 ET - \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  remains underweight on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets overall due to its positions in Asia, although it has reduced underweight positions in EMEA emerging markets, it says. The reason for a more positive view on EMEA emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is improved valuations after the recent selloff. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  has held overweight exposure in EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  only in Latin America but the "magnitude of exposure has been reduced given the souring U.S.-China trade relations and potential spillover effects from Argentina," \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 ET - \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is absorbing a government agency tasked with flagging larger-than-usual financial transactions in order to prevent money laundering, terrorism funding and mass-destruction weapons financing. The change was made official Monday night after days of speculation. The agency previously known as COAF has been assigned to different ministries earlier this year before landing at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It will now be called Financial Intelligence Unity, or UIF. The body has played an important role in the sprawling Carwash graft probe. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0812 ET - The eurozone is at "much higher risk" of suffering a recession than the U.S., despite downbeat market signals from the latter, says Unigestion. The eurozone has been close to recessionary levels for some time, according to the Swiss investment boutique's proprietary forecasts, dragged down by employment, consumption, and production, it says. Conversely, the U.S. economy continues to grow above potential levels - though at a lower pace - supported by investment, employment and consumption. The yield on 10-year Treasury bonds dropped below 2-year Treasuries for the first time since 2007, a pattern known as inversion. This is widely perceived as a harbinger of recession.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0755 ET - Higher bond rates dented any positive total return in euro-denominated investment-grade debt from the recent tightening in European corporate bond spreads, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . "While cash spreads finally joined the ongoing recovery in credit-default swaps, the move higher in rates ate up any positive total return impact in EUR IG," says Marco Stoeckle, head of credit research. European corporate-bond spreads have finally started tightening after bleak global economic data last week fueled fears of a looming recession, triggering a sell-off. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0731 ET - A recovery in risk sentiment should encourage more European companies to pile on cheaper debt, despite rising criticism from chief executive officers of companies rated at the lowest rung of the investment grade rating scale, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . "An extending recovery should see further issuers test the waters, although even CEOs of BBB-names are pointing to the potential side effects of ultra-depressed yields," the bank says. The German bank refers to recent comments from the chief executives of \uc2 Carlsberg\uc2  and \uc2 Valeo\uc2  slamming companies that feel the urge to rack up cheap debt, as a result of negative rates, to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0722 ET - Germany's upcoming ultra-long and very short government bond sales with no debt-servicing costs suggest that "Germany can loosen its purse strings and maintain the 'black zero' of its fiscal policy," says \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2  , chief market strategist at Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Wednesday, Germany will launch the new 0% August 2050 Bund with EUR2 billion, while next Tuesday it will sell EUR5 billion in new September 2021 Schatz. The coupon-setting on the Schatz is due next Monday but as the whole German yield curve is trading in negative levels, the coupon is set to be 0%. Chandler adds that the main deterrent of a more stimulative fiscal policy in Germany is not the means but the political will. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 20, 2019 09:01 ET (13:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190820ef8k001i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
