\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 5-Argentine markets gripped for second day by election-related fears\b0\par\par\uc2 1,087 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2019\par 02:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso closed weaker again on Tuesday following a second day of market turmoil triggered by opposition candidate Alberto Fernandez's landslide victory in a primary election that dealt a severe blow to President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's re-election chances.               \par \par \uc2 The peso closed 4.29% lower at 55.9 per U.S. dollar after touching 59 to the dollar earlier. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had hit an all-time low on Monday of 65 to the dollar, a drop of 30%, on fears that a Fernandez government could take \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic policies.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold $255 million of its own reserves since Monday to help steady the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 "The market thinks Fernandez will likely default and impose capital controls and renegotiate with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . In a nutshell, the market thinks Fernandez is the return of populism," said Claudio Irigoyen of \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  (BAML).               \par \par \uc2 Fernandez, who has former President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez\uc2  as his running mate, pulled off a stunning upset in the primary with a wider-than-expected 15-point lead over Macri, a free-market proponent.               \par \par \uc2 Monday's crash in the peso unnerved global equities investors, with markets already jittery over the Sino-U.S. trade war and protests in Hong Kong.               \par \par \uc2 "Yes, Argentina is a small economy. However, the last thing global markets want to see is another market-friendly government fall to populism and/or geopolitics," said \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  strategist Michael Every.               \par \par \uc2 A Brazilian hedge fund was among the casualties of Monday's market rout on Argentine assets, with at least 75% of its equity exposure, suffering losses between 25% and 59%.               \par \par \uc2 Newfoundland Capital Management of Sao Paulo was hit with a loss on paper of around $55 million, as its bets on four Argentine companies in agribusiness, natural gas, energy distribution and telecommunications imploded.               \par \par \uc2 The weaker peso quickly translated into higher prices on Argentine store shelves. Some shops selling household appliances in the capital Buenos Aires, at a loss for how to price their products amid the fluctuating exchange rate, stopped printing price tags and instead asked customers to inquire.               \par \par \uc2 There was also doubt over the future of an "essential goods" program introduced by Macri in April to fix low prices through October for 64 basic food products. The secretariate of trade did not respond to a request for comment on whether the government would renegotiate the prices with companies.               \par \par \uc2 Buenos Aires resident Florencia Nagy, 31, said, "The atmosphere has been tense" in the city.               \par \par \uc2 "A lot of anger, a lot of confusion, many people thinking that leaving the country could be an option," said Nagy, who said she voted for candidates from more than one party in the primary.               \par \par \uc2 BLAME GAME               \par \par \uc2 In an interview Monday, Fernandez said he was willing to cooperate with the current government after his primary triumph on Sunday sent the peso, stocks and bonds reeling.               \par \par \uc2 The primary results showed Fernandez, a former cabinet chief, was well placed to win October's general election in the first round. He blamed Macri for the market turmoil.               \par \par \uc2 "The dialogue is open, but I don't want to lie to Argentines. What can I do? I'm just a candidate. My pen doesn't sign decrees," Fernandez said in an interview with Argentine TV channel Net TV broadcast on Monday.               \par \par \uc2 Fernandez, regarded as a moderate within the Peronist movement, has said he would seek to rework Argentina's $57 billion standby agreement with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  if he won the general election. He has proposed an economic and social pact to combat inflation, which is running at 55%.               \par \par \uc2 Macri, too, deflected responsibility for the financial volatility, saying at a news conference on Monday that the opposition should "self-criticize" its own policies in the wake of the market reaction.               \par \par \uc2 Macri, a scion of one of Argentina's wealthiest families, came to power in 2015 on promises to kick-start Latin America's third-largest economy via a liberalization wave.               \par \par \uc2 But the promised recovery has not materialized and Argentina is in recession.                              \par \par \uc2 'JUST AN EXAMPLE'               \par \par \uc2 Though Argentines are accustomed to a decades-long cycle of peso weakness and inflation, the severity of this week's market reaction resonated on the streets of Buenos Aires, with some bracing for a long period of economic hardship.               \par \par \uc2 "If we get the same result (as the primary) in the October election, the country will be worse off," said Jazmin Lia, a 25-year-old student who said she voted for Macri in the primary.               \par \par \uc2 "What's happening with the exchange rate and the collapse of the market is just an example of what will happen," Lia added.               \par \par \uc2 Carolina Fiuza, 31, blamed Macri for the market volatility and said it seemed more like a "strategy than a reality." She said she was not worried about the market meltdown.               \par \par \uc2 "I see people desperate for the peso value to increase. I see everyone thinking that it will devalue even worse in October. In the end, I think everything will be work out, as always," said Fiuza, who voted for Peronist candidate Roberto Lavagna in the primaries.               \par \par \uc2 Argentine assets had not recorded the kind of simultaneous fall seen on Monday since the South American country's 2001 economic crisis and debt default, Refinitiv data showed.               \par \par \uc2 Investors were still assessing the damage caused by Monday's crash. Argentina's country risk rose 164 basis points to 1,631, the highest since 2009.               \par \par \uc2 Index provider MSCI said it has not yet considered reclassifying the recently upgraded Argentina stock index out of emerging markets despite the massive spike in volatility and decline in prices.               \par \par \uc2 Argentina's century bond fell over six points on Tuesday to trade at 51 cents on the dollar, compared with around 75 cents on Friday, according to MarketAxess data.               \par \par \uc2 As concerns rise about Argentina's ability to meet its debt obligations, investors are looking closely at the government's ability to roll over its peso maturities, including short-term notes known as 'Letes.'               \par \par \uc2 "Markets will be sweating bullets each time one of these maturities come due," Jeffries Fixed Income said in a note to investors.               \par \par \uc2 Argentina on Tuesday sold $409 million in 105-day Letes, according to a statement issued by the Treasury, adding that it had canceled a planned sale of 210-day paper. The government sometimes shortens the maturities of new debt sales in times of market upheaval.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-14T01:26:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190813ef8d030n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*