\par\par \b \uc2 This Week on the Frontiers, May 4th 2019\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Keeler \par \uc2 1,610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2019\par 00:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Africa\par \par \uc2 Leading sub-Saharan African economies struggle to maintain growth. Economic prospects for sub-Saharan Africa's key economies are dimming, with Angola, Zambia and Nigeria all struggling to make headway. According to a report by London-based \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , Angola's economy is likely to shrink this year as a recent rise in oil production eases. Nigeria has also seen an increase in oil output recently but the non-oil economy continues to struggle, the firm said.\par \par \uc2 Among the few bright spots is Kenya, where growth remained strong late last year, according to CapEc, but a continuing drought could prove a drag on the economy this year. Ghana also seems to be stabilizing, the report said, although growth in the country's economy, which is one of the region's recent outperformers, appears to be slowing, having dropped from 7.4% in the third quarter of 2018 to 6.8% in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 One longtime financial investor in sub-Saharan Africa told the Journal that opportunities in the region seem to be diminishing. "The lead of our Africa team is definitely risk-off at present -- he told me he just can't find opportunities that offer meaningful returns," the investor said.\par \par \uc2 African Development Bank\uc2  to support Nigeria's cocoa sector. The \uc2 African Development Bank\uc2  is to invest in Nigeria's cocoa and other agricultural sectors to boost production and create special agroprocessing zones in the country, Obafemi Oredein reports. Sayina Riman, president of the Cocoa Association of Nigeria, said the move is being spearheaded by the AfDB, which has agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Nigeria's cocoa sector as it did in the cocoa sectors in Ghana and C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire, the world's two largest producers of the commodity.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria is the world's fourth-largest cocoa producer but its production has fallen due to neglect of the sector, old and unproductive farms and farmers and poor funding by government, among other problems. Nigeria produces around 300,000 tons of cocoa, according to CAN, and moves to raise the output to 500,000 tons by government in the past eight years have failed.\par \par \uc2 Asia\par \par \uc2 Sri Lanka warns future attacks may be planned. Police say arrests and searches of dozens of locations linked to the perpetrators of the Easter bombings have turned up significant evidence that new attacks could be in the works, Saeed Shah and Uditha Jayasinghe report. Despite a state-of-emergency dragnet that has yielded over 100 arrests in 10 days, Sri Lankan authorities say they are deeply concerned that a second series of attacks could target places of worship and schools of the majority Sinhalese Buddhist community, which wasn't targeted in the initial attacks on Christian churches and major international tourist hotels.\par \par \uc2 Islamic State, the militant group originally based in Syria and Iraq, claimed responsibility for the synchronized attacks on Easter Sunday in this island nation of 21 million. The majority of Sri Lanka's population are Sinhalese Buddhists and there is a history of animosity between extremist members of the Buddhist and Muslim faiths here.\par \par \uc2 Security officials say the group formed by the attackers could have members who didn't take part in the Easter assault and are waiting to carry out further bombings.\par \par \uc2 Europe\par \par \uc2 Jaguar Land Rover\uc2  moves vehicle production to Slovakia. Carmaker Jaguar Land Rover, owned by India's \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2 , said it would be assembling the new Land Rover Defender at its plant in Slovakia, Anthony O. Goriainoff reports. The U.K.'s largest car manufacturer built the previous Defender model at its site in Solihull, England.\par \par \uc2 Vehicle production in Slovakia has been growing steadily with output this year expected to reach 1.15 million vehicles. Four major brands -- \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , \uc2 Jaguar Land Rover\uc2 , \uc2 Kia\uc2  and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  -- have established plants there, turning the small landlocked country into the world's biggest per-capita carmaker, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Latin America\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loosens rules for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would step in to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more readily to support the peso after several days of volatility last week sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging against the dollar, Jeffrey T. Lewis reports. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that under its new policy it would sell dollars into the market even if the exchange rate is stronger than 51.45 pesos to the dollar, which was the level the bank had previously set for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank said it would sell as much as $250 million if the peso weakens past the 51.45 level, and is ready to sell even more dollars if necessary to calm situations of excessive volatility. The peso strengthened more than 3% on Monday, to 44.89 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  was tight-lipped, but first deputy managing director \uc2 David Lipton\uc2  issued a brief and somewhat cryptic statement on Monday acknowledging the "recalibration of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies," but stopping short of explicitly approving of the move. The multilateral has a lot at stake: in October last year it entered into a $56 billion standby arrangement with the struggling South American nation.\par \par \uc2 Violence erupts in Venezuela as Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 calls for uprising. Thousands of Venezuelans clashed in the streets with pro-government forces on Tuesday following an audacious call by opposition leader \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2  to join him and a small group of military officers in overthrowing President Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro's government, Kejal Vyas reports. The violence ensued after Mr. Guaid\u243\'00\'F3, flanked by a small group of rebellious National Guard troops outside a Caracas military base, called on the public to help end a punishing economic and political crisis under Mr. Maduro.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 is viewed by the U.S. and more than 50 other nations as Venezuela's legitimate leader following a 2018 election that was marred by fraud allegations. Protests continued on Wednesday but failed to gain enough traction and by Thursday, the country's government began going after opposition leaders.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Trump, whose administration has said the Maduro regime is being protected by tens of thousands of Cuban troops, threatened Cuba with a "full and complete embargo" and "highest-level sanctions," if it continued its support.\par \par \uc2 Global\par \par \uc2 Frontier-market borrowing prompts concern. Debt levels in frontier markets continue to mount, raising concerns that countries might be overburdening themselves with loans. An analysis by the Institute of International Finance found frontier-market debt increased by more than $250 billion last year, pushing the total debt load to more than $3.2 trillion. The majority of the borrowing has been by the public sector, with government debt across the asset class reaching around 50% of GDP. Pakistan, Tunisia and Zambia's government debt grew particularly strongly in 2018.\par \par \uc2 According to the IIF, the rate of hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond issuance by frontier markets fell in the first quarter of this year, but the high level of borrowing that matures over the coming months is cause for concern. "Upcoming debt maturities will be onerous for some," the IIF's Khadija Mahmood and Juncheng Zhou said. "More than $125 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated frontier bonds and loans will mature through end-2020."\par \par \uc2 Citi economist David Lubin said a recent skew toward foreign-denominated borrowing and a dependence on commodities were creating potentially difficult conditions for frontier-market borrowers. "Frontier will keep looking risky without a very substantial boost to commodity prices, a very sharp decline in the dollar, or both," he said in a report last week.\par \par \uc2 Investors retreat as frontiers lag developed and emerging markets. Investors appear to be cooling on frontier markets as they continue to lose ground to more advanced markets, according to Citi frontier market analyst Andrew Howell. While developed markets rose by 3.4% in April and emerging markets tacked on more than 2%, the FM index fell by more than 1%, undercut by an 8.3% plunge in Argentina's market as well as a fall of 6.8% in Bangladesh and 5.7% in Nigeria, Mr. Howell noted.\par \par \uc2 Egypt, Romania and Morocco gained ground, but their rise was not enough to offset the other markets' declines. Investors appear to be wary of frontier markets, pulling some $219 million out of frontier-focused funds in March. "The $219m outflow, driven by active frontier funds, was the biggest this year and indicates that fundraising is off to a challenging start in 2019," Mr. Howell said.\par \par \uc2 KEY STORIES FROM THE WSJ\par \par \uc2 Sri Lanka Probes ISIS Links to Attacks\par \par \uc2 Senators Failed to End U.S. Support for Saudi-led War in Yemen\par \par \uc2 Iran Vows to Resist U.S. Oil Ban as Its Exports Decline\par