\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  has dollar firepower to battle election uncertainty -sources\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2019\par 19:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Feb 1 (Reuters) - Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sufficient dollar firepower to deal with a spike in demand for the greenback if uncertainty over the country's upcoming presidential election prompts another run on the peso, two sources at the bank said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Investors have returned this year to pesos as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilized after losing half its value against the dollar last year. However, there are fears this could reverse rapidly if business-friendly President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  fails to win a second term in October's general election.\par \par \uc2 Economic volatility is common during election years in Argentina, which has one of the highest inflation rates in the world at nearly 50 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 The stakes for the country are high. Macri's leadership, which has managed to calm markets after a tumultuous 2018, is pinning hopes on an economic revival this year to propel election hopes against the leftwing opposition.\par \par \uc2 Investors fears that if the country swings back to a Peronist president, this could lead to Macri's moves to open up the market being reversed and businesses once again looking to shift their money out of the country.\par \par \uc2 The bank source said Argentina has about $14 billion at the ready to respond to potential dollar demand, including around $10 billion that the finance ministry could sell during the year to pay expenses in pesos, and more than $3 billion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to operate in futures.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sources emphasized that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not anticipate coming up short if high dollar demand were to return.\par \par \uc2 "What we have to look at are the stocks in pesos that could be converted to dollars. When one looks at those stocks, it's a quite small amount," said one of the sources.\par \par \uc2 The higher demand for pesos since the end of 2018 strengthened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by almost 1 percent in January. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been making daily purchases of up to $50 million when the peso slips beyond the limits of a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band set as part of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $56.3 billion deal with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by purchasing about $560 million so far this year. (Reporting by Eliana Raszewski; writing by Cassandra Garrison; Editing by Dan Grebler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-2-1T19:21:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190201ef21038l9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
