\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2018\par 03:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 2108 ET - A severe drought gripping much of eastern Australia is unlikely to be eased by usual spring rains, the country's weather bureau says. The latest climate forecasts indicate conditions are likely to be drier and warmer than average over the coming months, meaning a low chance of recovery for drought-affected areas of eastern Australia, the Bureau of Meteorology says in its latest climate note. Weather conditions and sea temperatures over the Pacific and Indian Oceans, while currently neutral, point in modelling to a drought-worsening El Nino weather phenomenon forming in spring, the bureau says. One of the driest winters on record has seen crop forecasts lowered sharply, while some regional communities have dam levels at only 10% of capacity and may soon run out of water. (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor)\par \par \uc2 2106 ET - New Zealand expects to raise an extra NZ$80 million ($53.3 million) annually with a new NZ$35 levy on foreign tourists. The government has been mulling such a tax as tourist numbers overwhelm infrastructure and the environment. The country has in recent years been a vacation hotspot for wealthy Americans. While critics say the levy may harm tourism, which last year bypassed dairy as New Zealand's biggest export, Conservation Minister \uc2 Eugenie Sage\uc2  says the money will help "improve the protection and enhancement of New Zealand's distinctive natural environment and improve tourism planning." Almost 4 million tourists visited New Zealand the past year; the country's population is 4.7 million. (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor)\par \par \uc2 2018 ET - The key 24000 level for the Nikkei has given way for now, with the benchmark down an early 0.3% at 23960 following a late drop in the US after the latest interest-rate increase. Insurers are down following the overnight drop in bond yields and similar early decline in Japan, with \uc2 Dai-ichi Life\uc2  off 2%. Five-year JGB yields are down a half-basis point at -0.07%. Meanwhile, the dollar remains around Y112.75. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1947 ET - Japanese stocks are poised to start lower following a late-day selloff in the US after the Fed's latest rate hike. Nikkei futures opened down 0.3% at 23900 following yesterday's 8-month closing high and 8th-straight gain. Though the Nikkei has gotten back above 24000, what's key is whether what's been noted resistance this year can now be support. Meanwhile, the dollar is at Y112.75, compared to yesterday's Tokyo equity-markets close at Y112.88, and a fall in Treasury yields is liable to be followed by JGBs. Their yields have been climbing of late as well. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1932 ET - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sees a 30% chance of the next rate move for \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  being a cut. But for that to happen, the investment bank acknowledges the job market needs to deteriorate more than appears likely. Meanwhile, fiscal policy is likely to become gradually more supportive over time. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1855 ET [Dow Jones \}--\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos to 44 pesos to the US dollar. But if it breaches the upper limit of the so-called "non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  says the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell up to $150M to shore up the peso, which has depreciated sharply this year. The change in policy comes as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  agreed to increase its bailout program for \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after objecting to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  while calling for a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1830 ET [Dow Jones ] -- Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2 , says it could take a few months for inflation to start declining after the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped inflation targets in favor of monetary base goals to contain the supply of money. "We are attacking the root cause of inflation," Sandleris says. "The objective is that it will decline as fast as possible." The change in policy comes as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  boosted its bailout package for Argentina to $57.1B from $50B. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also committed to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos to 44 pesos to the USD, Sandleris says. \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a cause of conflict with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  , which supports a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1753 ET - Australian house prices are expected to remain in retreat until 2020, says ANZ. That's an extension of the big Aussie bank's earlier assumption that a slide, already in place for a more than a year, would bottom out in 2019. Further tightening of mortgage credit availability is likely, while the banks are expected to continue raising interest rates on home loans, it says. At some point, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will chime in with its own increases. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1748 ET - Governor Adrian Orr continues to have doubts about the economic outlook in New Zealand, despite recent robust GDP data and clear signs that business confidence is in recovery. The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  left its cash rate at 1.75% as expected and said it is unlikely to change through 2019 and into 2020. Crucially, the next move could be up or down. Still, there are signs core inflation is starting to rise, but the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  will look through it as it likely reflects a temporary rise in fuel prices. Global trade tensions are a threat to growth, he says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1649 ET - The peso firmed against the US dollar after the Fed raised interest rates as expected and reiterated its likely interest-rate path. The peso was quoted in Mexico City at 18.8580 to the dollar compared with 19.00 Tuesday, and local government debt rallied, pushing yields down. Banorte said Fed action could put pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  , which it expects will raise interest rates at next week's meeting. "In general the outlook of tighter US monetary conditions is a significant challenge for emerging markets, which could partially contaminate Mexican assets despite strong fundamentals," Banorte said. The IPC stock index slipped 0.1% to 49,606 points. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1618 ET - Shares of financial companies in the S&P 500 tumbled into the close, logging their biggest one-day loss since July. The S&P 500 financial sector ended the day down 1.3%, with heavyweight \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  losing 1.6% and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  shedding 1.8%. Partly to blame for the sector's selloff: a rally in Treasurys, which picked up steam after Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  concluded his press conference with some cautionary comments about stock valuations. The yield on the 10-year Treasury yield, which had been trading closer to 3.080% before the Fed released its policy statement, was recently at 3.050% and around its low for the day. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 1609 ET - A late-day selloff sends US stocks down on a day when the Fed announces its third rate increase this year and signals it would continue lifting them through next year to keep the economy on an even keel. Stocks held to a relatively steady range until the conclusion of Fed Chairman Powell's press conference, when they headed south into the close. A similar pattern appeared in the Treasury market as yields held relatively steady then dropped to 3.05% for the 10-year note late in the day, notching the biggest one-day decline since August. DJIA falls 106 points to 26385, the S&P loses 9 to 2905 and the Nasdaq drops 17 to 7990. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2018 21:08 ET (01:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180927ee9r00068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
