\par\par \b \uc2 Much-Weaker Job Climate Needed for NZ Rate Cut -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2018\par 01:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1932 ET - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sees a 30% chance of the next rate move for \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  being a cut. But for that to happen, the investment bank acknowledges the job market needs to deteriorate more than appears likely. Meanwhile, fiscal policy is likely to become gradually more supportive over time. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1855 ET [Dow Jones \}--\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos to 44 pesos to the US dollar. But if it breaches the upper limit of the so-called "non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  says the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell up to $150M to shore up the peso, which has depreciated sharply this year. The change in policy comes as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  agreed to increase its bailout program for \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after objecting to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  while calling for a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1830 ET [Dow Jones ] -- Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2 , says it could take a few months for inflation to start declining after the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped inflation targets in favor of monetary base goals to contain the supply of money. "We are attacking the root cause of inflation," Sandleris says. "The objective is that it will decline as fast as possible." The change in policy comes as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  boosted its bailout package for Argentina to $57.1B from $50B. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also committed to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos to 44 pesos to the USD, Sandleris says. \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a cause of conflict with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  , which supports a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1753 ET - Australian house prices are expected to remain in retreat until 2020, says ANZ. That's an extension of the big Aussie bank's earlier assumption that a slide, already in place for a more than a year, would bottom out in 2019. Further tightening of mortgage credit availability is likely, while the banks are expected to continue raising interest rates on home loans, it says. At some point, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will chime in with its own increases. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1748 ET - Governor Adrian Orr continues to have doubts about the economic outlook in New Zealand, despite recent robust GDP data and clear signs that business confidence is in recovery. The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  left its cash rate at 1.75% as expected and said it is unlikely to change through 2019 and into 2020. Crucially, the next move could be up or down. Still, there are signs core inflation is starting to rise, but the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  will look through it as it likely reflects a temporary rise in fuel prices. Global trade tensions are a threat to growth, he says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1505 ET - Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  takes on the Trump Administration's aggressive trade actions gingerly. "We are not responsible for trade policy and we don't comment on particular trade actions," Powell says at the press conference. But he notes "we have been hearing a rising chorus of concern" even as their hasn't been as much impact in actual economic data so far. Powell says an outcome with lower tariffs would be good for the economy. But if a lot of barriers go up and stay there, then "that's going to be bad" for the American economy. He also says on the prospects tariffs boosting inflation: "You could see prices moving up. You don't see it yet" but the tariffs could give firms cover to boost prices. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1445 ET - In his press conference after the FOMC meeting, Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  was asked about whether President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's criticism of rate rises are a problem. Powell doesn't say the president's name and falls back on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boilerplate, noting "we don't consider political factors or things like that" when making policy. Instead, he says the Fed focuses on its mission. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1415 ET - \uc2 US government\uc2  bond yields tick lower, then pare their declines, after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raises short-term interest rates and removes the word "accommodative" from its policy statement. Investors had been widely expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates in September, but had been less sure of whether the Fed would signal a more aggressive approach to future rate increases. That had driven yields higher heading into Wednesday's meeting, although traders said on first glance, the Fed's latest policy statement didn't strike them as being hawkish. The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  note fell to around 3.065% before recently trading at 3.078%, compared with 3.102% Tuesday. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 1411 ET - The Fed's interest rate forecasts may not hold any great surprises relative to what was seen in June, but it's notable that central bankers see an extended period of time of running monetary policy above its long run level. The Fed may be dispensing with descriptions of policy as "accommodative" and Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  may be pushed the concept of neutral rates off to the margin. Still, against the Fed's estimate of a long-term fed funds target rate of 3%, they see monetary policy delivering rates above that in 2019, 2020 and 2021. Given the duration of the business cycle, trade war rumblings and the worries about the yield curve, it will be very interesting to see if the Fed can deliver on such a hawkish policy outlook. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1410 ET - The September Federal Reserve policy statement showed no change in officials' characterization of the economy and its strength compared with the previous policy statement. Officials said the labor market has continued to strengthen, while the economy has been rising at a strong pace. Meanwhile, inflation remains near the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target. The main change in September's statement, other than the rate increase decision, was the drop of "accommodative" in the description of interest rates. (sarah.chaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1407 ET - Twelve of 16 Fed officials now expect four interest rate increases this year, more than in previous months, according to new economic projections. In June, eight Fed officials expected at least four rate rises in 2018. The higher number of officials projecting four rate bumps signals a stronger belief among officials that the economy is running strong, necessitating gradual rate increases to keep it from overheating. (sarah.chaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1404 ET - Federal Reserve officials removed language in their policy statement that had described short-term interest rates as "accommodative," meaning they are still low enough to stimulate economic growth. Most officials think rates are still accommodative but are becoming less so with each rate increase, and are on track to reach a neutral level that neither spurs nor slows growth in coming months. The problem is officials' aren't sure where neutral lies or when they'll get there. Waiting until later this year or next year to drop the phrase would have risked sending an unintended message then that they agreed they were at neutral, which they are unlikely to do, given the uncertainties involved. (sarah.chaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2018 19:32 ET (23:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180926ee9q003gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
